-♪ La-la-la ♪

23
00:01:09,170 --> 00:01:12,073
♪ Then repeat to yourself
It's just a show ♪

24
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:14,642
♪ I should really just relax ♪

25
00:01:15,008 --> 00:01:20,047
♪ For Mystery Science Theater 3000! ♪

26
00:01:30,825 --> 00:01:32,960
Hi, everybody.
Welcome to the Satellite of Love.

27
00:01:33,027 --> 00:01:35,163
My name's Joel Robinson,
and my buddy, here,

28
00:01:35,230 --> 00:01:39,700
Crow T. Robot's fast asleep,
and through the magic of Cambot,

29
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:42,037
we're going to be able to see and monitor

30
00:01:42,103 --> 00:01:43,638
-what he's dreaming about.
-[snoring]

31
00:01:43,705 --> 00:01:46,740
Assisting me today is Nurse Tom Servo.

32
00:01:46,808 --> 00:01:49,743
Joel, this nurse's outfit makes me
very self-conscious and embarrassed,

33
00:01:49,810 --> 00:01:52,213
and yet, I don't seem to mind.

34
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:55,217
Weird. Yeah, that's what it is. Weird.

35
00:01:55,283 --> 00:01:57,852
Well, never mind that
because we're about to hook up Crow

36
00:01:57,918 --> 00:02:00,053
-and see what he's dreaming.
-Ooh.

37
00:02:01,022 --> 00:02:04,458
Whoo-hoo! Oh, ho! Oh, boy!

38
00:02:04,525 --> 00:02:05,560
Oh. Hey.

39
00:02:05,627 --> 00:02:08,497
Looks like we're due
for another almond bark rainstorm.

40
00:02:08,563 --> 00:02:11,332
[Joel] Well, looks like a pretty healthy
normal dream for a bot

41
00:02:11,398 --> 00:02:12,733
his age to be having.

42
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:14,135
[Tom] Well, looks pretty normal to me.

43
00:02:14,202 --> 00:02:16,370
Ha-ha-ha! Whoo-hoo!

44
00:02:16,437 --> 00:02:18,640
Hey, big boy.

45
00:02:18,707 --> 00:02:22,443
I tell you, that Tom Servo's
really something else.

46
00:02:22,643 --> 00:02:23,778
Oh...

47
00:02:24,145 --> 00:02:26,848
[Tom] Turn it off! Turn it off!

48
00:02:27,015 --> 00:02:30,218
[yelling] Turn it off!
[panting]

49
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:33,822
Whoa! Whoo!

50
00:02:33,888 --> 00:02:35,122
Oh, hi, Joel.

51
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:36,958
Boy, I had a weird dream.

52
00:02:37,025 --> 00:02:39,960
I was on a ship, and--
Oh, hi, Tom.

53
00:02:40,027 --> 00:02:41,628
And it was really strange 'cause--

54
00:02:42,997 --> 00:02:46,667
[screaming]

55
00:02:52,607 --> 00:02:55,610
Come on, Tom.
I wasn't really even dreaming about you.

56
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:58,813
I was just seeing the part
of myself that is you.

57
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,848
Uh... dressed as a candy striper.
Ha-ha!

58
00:03:01,917 --> 00:03:03,685
I could never trust you, again.

59
00:03:03,752 --> 00:03:04,752
I'm sorry.

60
00:03:05,453 --> 00:03:07,822
-You do look cute, though.
-Really?

61
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:11,025
Oh, come on, you two.
Sturm and Drang are calling, here on the--

62
00:03:11,092 --> 00:03:12,827
-You really think so?
-Yeah, I do.

63
00:03:12,893 --> 00:03:17,365
Frank, so help me, this time I'm really--
Oh, hello, Joel.

64
00:03:17,432 --> 00:03:18,432
It's been a rough week.

65
00:03:18,498 --> 00:03:19,867
Frank's been bad again.

66
00:03:19,933 --> 00:03:21,602
Hey, it's who I am, all right?

67
00:03:21,668 --> 00:03:23,203
Oh, Frank.

68
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:24,638
Joel, you've seen me with Frank.

69
00:03:24,705 --> 00:03:27,075
You've seen me trust him,
give him free rein.

70
00:03:27,142 --> 00:03:28,275
It just doesn't work.

71
00:03:28,342 --> 00:03:30,043
Frank just takes advantage.

72
00:03:30,110 --> 00:03:32,613
Oh, I'm really sorry about that.

73
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:35,048
Oh, I wish I could believe that.

74
00:03:35,115 --> 00:03:37,652
Fortunately, my invention this week

75
00:03:37,718 --> 00:03:39,653
is exactly what's called for.

76
00:03:39,753 --> 00:03:43,323
Joel, this is the tough love seat.

77
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:46,860
With its fine, sticky Naugahyde
and its sharp studs

78
00:03:46,928 --> 00:03:51,032
on the seat back inside,
one day in the tough love seat

79
00:03:51,098 --> 00:03:52,833
provides all the nurturing punishment

80
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:55,068
of an entire year at military school.

81
00:03:55,135 --> 00:03:56,303
Hey, I do what I do.

82
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:57,972
You don't have to like it, all right?

83
00:03:58,038 --> 00:04:00,475
Oh, what now? Am I supposed to
get into your little seat?

84
00:04:00,542 --> 00:04:01,943
Is that what this is all about?

85
00:04:02,010 --> 00:04:04,545
Oh, that would be fine, Frank,
but before you do

86
00:04:04,612 --> 00:04:07,748
notice how this stain is still fresh.

87
00:04:07,815 --> 00:04:09,917
Oh, now that I've seen that, I'm

88
00:04:09,983 --> 00:04:12,320
totally going to change the way I live.

89
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:13,855
Leg up!

90
00:04:15,188 --> 00:04:16,890
Clips in place.

91
00:04:17,025 --> 00:04:18,727
Oh, and I almost forgot.

92
00:04:19,393 --> 00:04:22,863
This little devil here provides 110 volts

93
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:26,600
of current surging intricately
throughout the Naugahyde.

94
00:04:27,168 --> 00:04:33,173
[electricity buzzing]

95
00:04:33,373 --> 00:04:36,910
It's a simple lesson, Frank.
Actions have consequences.

96
00:04:36,978 --> 00:04:38,412
Tell me about it.

97
00:04:38,612 --> 00:04:43,350
And finally, Joel,
since there is room for two,

98
00:04:43,417 --> 00:04:45,453
I can join Frank.

99
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:51,025
Perhaps we can start that long
and painful process of healing together.

100
00:04:51,092 --> 00:04:53,327
[electricity buzzing]

101
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:57,798
Remember, Frank, this hurts me
as much as it hurts you.

102
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:01,302
Why don't you go head, Joel.

103
00:05:01,368 --> 00:05:04,105
Ahhhhhhhhhh.

104
00:05:04,572 --> 00:05:06,440
Whoa.
Wonder what Frank did.

105
00:05:06,507 --> 00:05:09,977
Yeah, I don't know, I think
some punishment can border on abuse.

106
00:05:10,043 --> 00:05:11,678
Anyway, this week's invention exchange

107
00:05:11,745 --> 00:05:15,450
is based on popular trendspotter
Faith Popcorn, author

108
00:05:15,517 --> 00:05:17,285
of the popular Popcorn Report.

109
00:05:17,352 --> 00:05:19,220
She's hotter than ever these days.

110
00:05:19,287 --> 00:05:21,255
And there are more and more trends
every day,

111
00:05:21,322 --> 00:05:23,792
and it's critical that we all know
about them and react.

112
00:05:23,858 --> 00:05:27,762
Right. And that's why we've come up
with Microwave Faith Popcorn.

113
00:05:27,828 --> 00:05:30,732
Now you can get her dead-on
and uncanny trendspotting

114
00:05:30,798 --> 00:05:32,233
in a matter of seconds.

115
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:34,368
Hey, what's the next trend going to be?

116
00:05:34,435 --> 00:05:37,605
Neo-fascism? Bank failures?
Self-surgery?

117
00:05:37,672 --> 00:05:38,740
It doesn't matter.

118
00:05:38,807 --> 00:05:40,675
You see, with Microwave Faith Popcorn,

119
00:05:40,742 --> 00:05:43,543
you'll never be blindsided
by pop culture again.

120
00:05:43,610 --> 00:05:45,413
And it's ready in just seconds.

121
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:47,548
-[microwave bell]
-Oh, wow.

122
00:05:47,615 --> 00:05:48,648
-Smells great.
-Yeah, let's see.

123
00:05:48,717 --> 00:05:51,385
Oh, here's a hot one. Let's see.
It says, huh,

124
00:05:51,452 --> 00:05:57,258
"Underwear will be replaced
with the advent of spray-on skin liners."

125
00:05:57,325 --> 00:05:58,492
What do you know?

126
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:00,595
-Hey, let me see one.
-OK.

127
00:06:01,295 --> 00:06:03,030
Oh, ha. "Grandpa-tossing.

128
00:06:03,163 --> 00:06:05,332
Fun, low-cost, join a league!"

129
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:07,102
Hmm, let me try one of them
buttered trends, there.

130
00:06:07,168 --> 00:06:09,070
OK. Let's see. OK. Here.

131
00:06:09,603 --> 00:06:12,640
"Bras for businessmen
no longer just for a select few."

132
00:06:12,707 --> 00:06:14,208
-Wow.
-Makes sense.

133
00:06:14,275 --> 00:06:17,245
What's this?
Here's one that didn't quite pop.

134
00:06:17,312 --> 00:06:18,312
What's that one say?

135
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:20,313
"Feminist Catholicism."

136
00:06:20,448 --> 00:06:22,417
-Hmm.
-Oh, didn't pop.

137
00:06:22,483 --> 00:06:23,483
No, it didn't.

138
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:24,985
What do you think, sirs?

139
00:06:26,387 --> 00:06:29,557
Remember, Frank, I'm doing this for you.

140
00:06:30,492 --> 00:06:32,927
I know that now, Doctor.

141
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:37,232
[electricity buzzing]

142
00:06:41,335 --> 00:06:42,670
Well, Joel.

143
00:06:42,737 --> 00:06:45,707
Now that Frank and I have
achieved a new beginning,

144
00:06:45,773 --> 00:06:49,777
I can tell you that
your experiment this week,

145
00:06:49,843 --> 00:06:53,680
Bride of the Monster,
comes direct from one of Hollywood's

146
00:06:53,747 --> 00:06:56,350
most famous cross-dressing directors.

147
00:06:56,417 --> 00:06:59,653
-Penny Marshall?
-No! Ed Wood, Jr.

148
00:06:59,753 --> 00:07:05,058
And it's preceded
by a bad, bad, bad short: Hired.

149
00:07:05,125 --> 00:07:07,628
It's about work.
Hope you like it.

150
00:07:07,695 --> 00:07:09,330
Send them the movie, Frank.

151
00:07:10,765 --> 00:07:14,135
[all] "Jerry Reed elected to high office!"

152
00:07:14,202 --> 00:07:15,235
-[alarm sounds]
-Ahh!

153
00:07:15,302 --> 00:07:16,870
Movie sign!

154
00:07:28,817 --> 00:07:30,685
-[Crow] Oh, wow.
-[Joel] Oh, man. Jerry Lee.

155
00:07:30,752 --> 00:07:31,752
Who'd have thought?

156
00:07:31,818 --> 00:07:34,355
[Joel] Hey, isn't that
the John Belushi biography?

157
00:07:34,388 --> 00:07:36,823
Well, that's something you didn't hear
much during the Bush Administration.

158
00:07:36,890 --> 00:07:39,960
-[Tom] The sequel was Hired 2: Laid Off.
-[Joel] Yeah.

159
00:07:40,728 --> 00:07:43,163
-[Tom] Jam Handy Organization.
-[Joel] To the rescue.

160
00:07:43,230 --> 00:07:44,698
[Tom] Keep your jam handy.

161
00:07:44,765 --> 00:07:46,933
Jimmy, you want to make a big success
selling Chevrolets.

162
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,335
[Joel] Sure, we all do.

163
00:07:48,402 --> 00:07:50,337
-I think you've got what it takes.
-[Tom] Brill Cream.

164
00:07:50,403 --> 00:07:53,740
You joined us with a fine reputation
as a salesman in other lines.

165
00:07:53,842 --> 00:07:55,510
Now, get back on your side of the desk.

166
00:07:55,577 --> 00:07:58,312
I've never sold Chevrolets before,
but I believe I can do it.

167
00:07:58,378 --> 00:08:00,515
- [Joel] Yeah, right.
-I'm sure you can, Jimmy.

168
00:08:00,815 --> 00:08:04,052
And I want you to know that I picked you
as the most likely to succeed out

169
00:08:04,118 --> 00:08:06,153
of a number of young men
we've been considering to fill

170
00:08:06,253 --> 00:08:08,255
-this opening in our sales organization.
- [Joel] Steak.

171
00:08:08,322 --> 00:08:10,023
That's mighty flattering, Mr. Warren.

172
00:08:10,090 --> 00:08:12,560
I'm going to do everything I can
to try to prove you right.

173
00:08:12,627 --> 00:08:13,962
- [Joel] Which isn't much.
-I wanted to get started

174
00:08:14,028 --> 00:08:15,663
just as soon as I can, too.

175
00:08:15,730 --> 00:08:18,900
-Do I start selling right away?
-Ha-ha! In that suit?

176
00:08:18,967 --> 00:08:20,635
First you've got to know your product.

177
00:08:21,635 --> 00:08:24,938
Here's some things you want to do
before you even think about contacting.

178
00:08:25,005 --> 00:08:27,442
[Joel] Here, put on this jewelry.

179
00:08:28,275 --> 00:08:31,378
[Tom] This is when I was in Pep Club,
and here's my senior picture.

180
00:08:32,513 --> 00:08:33,915
Why does he have to
read The Joy Luck Club?"

181
00:08:37,083 --> 00:08:40,453
[Joel] Ah. My failure
looks more and more imminent.

182
00:08:41,055 --> 00:08:43,157
[Tom] Cop Killer.
Oh, this sounds like fun.

183
00:08:45,125 --> 00:08:47,228
Now he has to read his boss's prose!

184
00:08:47,762 --> 00:08:51,698
[Joel] If you flip it real fast,
the car seems to move.

185
00:08:51,765 --> 00:08:54,302
[Tom] Oh, now, there's a pretty
all-encompassing book.

186
00:08:54,368 --> 00:08:56,137
He knows what's what, now.

187
00:08:57,472 --> 00:09:00,808
Honey, I got a job,
can I come in, please?

188
00:09:01,808 --> 00:09:04,045
[Joel] Ah, can I use your bathroom?

189
00:09:07,348 --> 00:09:10,250
[Tom] Aw, come on, Dad. It's not insurance
this time. It's really neat.

190
00:09:10,317 --> 00:09:11,585
It's cars!

191
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:16,090
This is the front door.
You were just at the back door.

192
00:09:17,658 --> 00:09:21,562
[Joel] Ah, guess I shouldn't be trying
to sell cars in Amish country.

193
00:09:21,862 --> 00:09:24,698
[Tom] Not going away this time.
I could stand here all day.

194
00:09:27,402 --> 00:09:28,835
[Joel] Good, my taxi.

195
00:09:28,970 --> 00:09:31,172
[Tom] Oh, um, good morning sir, or madam,

196
00:09:31,338 --> 00:09:34,275
I love your nice chatea-- chapeau--
Oh, pfft.

197
00:09:34,342 --> 00:09:37,878
I-- just bludgeoned my husband.
I really don't have time, thank you.

198
00:09:37,945 --> 00:09:40,782
-Thank you.
-[Tom] Good time to case the joint.

199
00:09:42,350 --> 00:09:45,887
[Joel] Darn. That's what I need--
a car to show.

200
00:09:46,487 --> 00:09:48,855
None of these prospects seem much good.

201
00:09:48,922 --> 00:09:50,658
So, time for booze.

202
00:09:50,725 --> 00:09:52,960
Well, they're the best I've got,
so I'll stay with them.

203
00:09:53,027 --> 00:09:54,462
What a loser.

204
00:09:55,630 --> 00:09:57,665
[Tom] Oh, no, you see,
it's called a "car".

205
00:09:57,732 --> 00:09:59,933
[Joel] I just want
to read you one of my poems.

206
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,102
It's Dear Eliza. It's--

207
00:10:02,168 --> 00:10:05,405
I know you don't have
a license, but look at this.

208
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:09,077
[Tom] Now this one's really amusing.
Garfield pulls at Odie's tongue.

209
00:10:09,143 --> 00:10:10,712
I love this.

210
00:10:11,678 --> 00:10:14,515
Yeah, I thought I knew
something about trucks.

211
00:10:14,582 --> 00:10:18,052
-[Joel] But I was living a horrible lie.
-Three models seem to fit his job.

212
00:10:18,118 --> 00:10:20,087
I didn't know which one to concentrate on.

213
00:10:20,253 --> 00:10:21,922
-Well, you can't sell them all.
-[Tom] May I help you, sir?

214
00:10:21,988 --> 00:10:25,292
No, but I'd hate to think that sale
I made to old man Simmons

215
00:10:25,358 --> 00:10:28,195
day before yesterday is going
to have to last me all month.

216
00:10:28,262 --> 00:10:30,998
[Tom] That's nice.
Maybe you'd just like to browse the place.

217
00:10:31,265 --> 00:10:33,800
I just don't seem to get
the hang of this business.

218
00:10:33,867 --> 00:10:35,402
[Crow] The wipers work.

219
00:10:35,803 --> 00:10:38,405
[Tom] Oh. No, no.
I don't want to buy a car.

220
00:10:38,472 --> 00:10:41,042
[Joel] Wait a minute, wait a minute.
Can I tell you about the rear wheel drive?

221
00:10:41,108 --> 00:10:42,643
[Tom] How many ways can I say no?

222
00:10:42,710 --> 00:10:46,380
Wow, this one's about
a priest, a minister, and a rabbi, see...

223
00:10:46,447 --> 00:10:49,217
[Joel] No, you're telling it wrong.

224
00:10:49,283 --> 00:10:51,885
You see, Mr. Brooks,
the Chevrolet knee-action assembly

225
00:10:51,952 --> 00:10:53,653
is different from the Plymouth unit.

226
00:10:53,753 --> 00:10:56,323
I understand the Plymouth
has the most expensive construction

227
00:10:56,390 --> 00:10:58,325
-of any car.
- [Joel] Oh, you got me.

228
00:10:58,392 --> 00:11:00,327
What's the difference
in the two knee actions?

229
00:11:00,393 --> 00:11:05,165
Well, I don't know the difference,
but I do know that Chevrolets is better.

230
00:11:05,232 --> 00:11:06,667
[Joel] What a dork!

231
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:09,337
[Tom] That was the most
humiliating afternoon I ever had.

232
00:11:09,403 --> 00:11:12,072
I can't believe I wet on his couch.

233
00:11:12,138 --> 00:11:14,908
Maybe I should not have
dressed as the Angel of Death.

234
00:11:15,943 --> 00:11:18,712
[Joel] Hey, nobody buy a car,
pass it down.

235
00:11:18,778 --> 00:11:20,782
-[Tom] Nobody buy a car.
-Nobody buys a car.

236
00:11:20,982 --> 00:11:22,115
[Tom] No cars.

237
00:11:22,483 --> 00:11:27,153
♪ I have often walked
Down this street before ♪

238
00:11:28,255 --> 00:11:31,858
Submitted for you approval--
a salesman who has no color.

239
00:11:31,925 --> 00:11:33,760
[Joel] I could off myself.

240
00:11:34,162 --> 00:11:35,797
There's a call on Beacon Street.

241
00:11:35,863 --> 00:11:37,732
[Tom] I'd probably fail there too.

242
00:11:37,798 --> 00:11:39,100
It's way over on the other side of town.

243
00:11:39,167 --> 00:11:41,035
I don't have a car. Hey!

244
00:11:41,102 --> 00:11:43,037
Well, might just as well
go after it right now.

245
00:11:43,137 --> 00:11:45,138
[Joel] Oh, look, there are, like,
signs on every door now.

246
00:11:45,238 --> 00:11:47,808
"We have a car."
"We have a car."

247
00:11:47,875 --> 00:11:50,077
[Tom] Hey, Edna Ferber
and Joseph Kennedy.

248
00:11:50,143 --> 00:11:51,612
And then I became an Eagle Scout.

249
00:11:51,678 --> 00:11:54,382
But enough about me, OK, um--

250
00:11:55,215 --> 00:11:56,717
[Tom] Could you turn down the music?

251
00:11:56,783 --> 00:11:58,152
I can't hear a thing you're saying.

252
00:12:00,153 --> 00:12:05,092
But, Mr. Herrington, $390 is just
the absolute best we can do on your car.

253
00:12:05,158 --> 00:12:07,695
And that's exactly the reason
why I won't buy.

254
00:12:07,762 --> 00:12:08,762
You're too far out of line.

255
00:12:08,828 --> 00:12:12,065
[Joel] I knew you'd try
to make me pay money.

256
00:12:12,833 --> 00:12:16,103
But Jimmy, we've got five
'39 4 or 2-doors on the lot now.

257
00:12:16,170 --> 00:12:19,473
Don't you think I know that?
I live with that every day of my life.

258
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:21,942
We don't need '39 2-doors.

259
00:12:22,008 --> 00:12:24,812
OK, I see your side of it.

260
00:12:24,878 --> 00:12:28,582
I suppose if I sat on my butt
all day, I'd things that way too.

261
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:33,053
[Tom] Hmm.
I didn't know he had a prison record.

262
00:12:33,087 --> 00:12:34,988
Says here he's wanted in Idaho.

263
00:12:35,957 --> 00:12:37,892
[Joel] What in the world?

264
00:12:37,958 --> 00:12:39,860
Assault with a deadly weapon?

265
00:12:40,293 --> 00:12:44,798
AKA the pants-less salesman
with a piddling peddler?

266
00:12:45,365 --> 00:12:49,068
[Joel] What the--?
He got a "D" in trigonometry? My--

267
00:12:49,137 --> 00:12:50,437
Oh.

268
00:12:50,503 --> 00:12:53,173
[Tom] Well, but he is a Pisces
with a moon in Aries.

269
00:12:54,342 --> 00:12:56,810
Eh, I'd fire him if he weren't my son.

270
00:12:56,877 --> 00:13:00,780
[Joel squeaking]

271
00:13:01,482 --> 00:13:03,217
Are you there, Lord?

272
00:13:03,283 --> 00:13:04,518
[Tom] Hello, wall.

273
00:13:04,952 --> 00:13:08,322
Well, there is a blue steak and a pitcher
of martinis with my name on it.

274
00:13:11,892 --> 00:13:13,293
[Tom] That's nearly impossible.

275
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:16,363
No human under my employ
has ever sold a car.

276
00:13:16,430 --> 00:13:19,600
[Joel] Well, I guess no one
wins the Mathis album.

277
00:13:25,005 --> 00:13:28,008
[Crow] If I could read,
I'd know what the problem is.

278
00:13:28,675 --> 00:13:32,345
[Tom] Oh, there's the problem.
I forgot to carry the two.

279
00:13:33,013 --> 00:13:35,415
[Joel] Willard Scott?

280
00:13:38,318 --> 00:13:42,022
Uh... Dad, you're smoking
my kid's bubble pipe.

281
00:13:44,123 --> 00:13:46,360
[Joel coughs] Nice night for it.

282
00:13:47,995 --> 00:13:51,098
[Tom] I think it's time for you
to move out on your own, son.

283
00:13:53,967 --> 00:13:58,538
Well, at least he's got
a nice wife to go home-- Oh, awkward.

284
00:14:00,107 --> 00:14:01,808
[Joel] Moon for the Misbegotten.

285
00:14:01,875 --> 00:14:03,543
Death of a Sales Manager.

286
00:14:05,045 --> 00:14:06,413
[Tom humming]

287
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:07,548
♪ Sandy Frank ♪

288
00:14:07,615 --> 00:14:09,383
You're looking beefier, son.

289
00:14:11,452 --> 00:14:13,987
I thought you two boys
might like some refreshments,

290
00:14:14,087 --> 00:14:16,490
so here's a pitcher of nice cold--

291
00:14:16,623 --> 00:14:18,858
- [Joel] Stoli?
-Lemonade.

292
00:14:18,925 --> 00:14:21,862
-[Joel grumbles]
-Thanks, Mother.

293
00:14:21,928 --> 00:14:24,832
[Tom] And tonight we'll have
some nice, hot cuddling.

294
00:14:24,898 --> 00:14:27,568
Ahh! Gravity at work.

295
00:14:28,735 --> 00:14:30,203
[Joel] Who's the doughy man
on our porch steps?

296
00:14:34,475 --> 00:14:37,043
[Joel] Well, I'll go deep fry something.

297
00:14:38,945 --> 00:14:43,017
I'll be upstairs in my burlap teddy.

298
00:14:43,083 --> 00:14:46,020
Got something might important
on your mind these days.

299
00:14:46,087 --> 00:14:47,187
Haven't you, son?

300
00:14:47,253 --> 00:14:48,422
[Tom] Am I that transparent, Dad?

301
00:14:48,488 --> 00:14:50,623
-No, not especially.
- [Joel] So, lay off!

302
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:51,992
Just the usual things.

303
00:14:52,058 --> 00:14:53,393
Crushing puppies.

304
00:14:53,460 --> 00:14:54,828
Business, you know.

305
00:14:56,363 --> 00:14:57,530
Sales problems.

306
00:14:57,598 --> 00:14:58,698
Salesmen.

307
00:14:58,765 --> 00:15:01,235
Now, don't try to fool your old dad.

308
00:15:01,302 --> 00:15:03,937
There's something extra special
worrying you,

309
00:15:04,003 --> 00:15:05,438
or I missed my guess.

310
00:15:05,505 --> 00:15:07,842
[Tom] You've killed again,
haven't you, son?

311
00:15:08,875 --> 00:15:10,810
-You know, I was just--
-[all] Ahh!

312
00:15:11,412 --> 00:15:13,547
Take the average young fellow, today.

313
00:15:13,613 --> 00:15:15,848
He won't get out and work the way
we used to when I started to sell.

314
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:17,483
[Joel] Work it, work it, uh-huh.

315
00:15:17,550 --> 00:15:19,387
What makes you think that?

316
00:15:19,453 --> 00:15:21,555
Well, take this young fellow,
Jimmy Hill, for example.

317
00:15:21,588 --> 00:15:22,790
[Crow] OK!

318
00:15:22,857 --> 00:15:25,592
Last month, when I was looking
for another new car salesman,

319
00:15:25,658 --> 00:15:29,430
he looked to me to have twice as much
on the bowl as anybody else I could find.

320
00:15:29,530 --> 00:15:31,832
He looked like a pretty
good boy to me, too.

321
00:15:31,898 --> 00:15:33,767
[Joel] That's right. Testify, son.

322
00:15:33,833 --> 00:15:36,337
Oh, yes, he makes a sale now and then.

323
00:15:36,670 --> 00:15:39,807
But that isn't the kind of a man I need.
Or want.

324
00:15:39,873 --> 00:15:43,377
Hasn't the man learned
the knack of selling, I suppose.

325
00:15:43,443 --> 00:15:45,078
- [Joel] Huh?
-Well, I guess not.

326
00:15:45,145 --> 00:15:46,680
Hey, keep it down over there.

327
00:15:46,747 --> 00:15:49,248
Don't you know what he's doing wrong?

328
00:15:49,317 --> 00:15:51,385
I can see enough from his sales reports.

329
00:15:51,518 --> 00:15:53,953
Mmm, pretty much on his own, then.

330
00:15:54,053 --> 00:15:55,655
[Tom] Oh, you blame me, huh?

331
00:15:55,990 --> 00:15:58,492
But he's had a good,
thorough basic training.

332
00:15:58,558 --> 00:16:01,862
I haven't got the time to play nursemaid
to any of the salesmen.

333
00:16:01,962 --> 00:16:03,830
Maybe not, son.

334
00:16:03,897 --> 00:16:06,267
But I wouldn't know about that.

335
00:16:06,333 --> 00:16:08,368
That's how you handle a salesman.

336
00:16:08,435 --> 00:16:10,537
That isn't what Harry Carpenter told me

337
00:16:10,603 --> 00:16:13,173
when you started out
to work for him 11 years ago.

338
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:15,908
-J'accuse!
-But I got out and worked.

339
00:16:15,975 --> 00:16:16,977
Hard.

340
00:16:17,043 --> 00:16:19,480
And that's what
I'm concerned about right now.

341
00:16:19,547 --> 00:16:21,948
Young fellows today won't work
the way we did 11 years ago.

342
00:16:22,015 --> 00:16:25,018
-Hard.
-Maybe not, son.

343
00:16:25,085 --> 00:16:28,822
And then again, maybe they're
working harder than you think.

344
00:16:30,157 --> 00:16:32,727
-[Joel] Oh, food for thought.
-[Tom] What do you know, huh?

345
00:16:32,793 --> 00:16:34,762
I'll ruminate on that one.

346
00:16:44,405 --> 00:16:48,908
[thunder]

347
00:16:49,710 --> 00:16:53,747
Ha! Bella Abzug is actually
a lot scarier than Bela Lugosi.

348
00:16:53,813 --> 00:16:55,348
- [Joel] Bigger, too.
-[Tom] Tor Johnson?

349
00:16:55,415 --> 00:16:58,152
Time for going to bed!

350
00:16:58,218 --> 00:17:00,753
[Joel] Bram Stoker's Bride of the Monster.

351
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:02,688
I wonder where she's registered.

352
00:17:04,323 --> 00:17:06,693
-[Tom] Big Tony McCoy.
- [Joel] Harvey B. Dunn?

353
00:17:06,760 --> 00:17:08,895
That's the sequel to Johnny Be Good.

354
00:17:08,962 --> 00:17:10,097
Harvey B. Dunn.

355
00:17:11,998 --> 00:17:14,535
Hey, which of Shatner's Becwar
novels did you like best?

356
00:17:14,602 --> 00:17:16,937
-[Tom] I like the second one.
- [Joel] That's TekWars.

357
00:17:17,570 --> 00:17:19,973
Ben Frommer, guide to Transylvania?

358
00:17:22,008 --> 00:17:23,977
I wonder who did his gowns for this one.

359
00:17:27,613 --> 00:17:29,650
[Tom] Hey, did you notice no Hatfields?

360
00:17:29,717 --> 00:17:31,585
Huh? Huh?

361
00:17:32,685 --> 00:17:33,687
I'm dead, Jim.

362
00:17:37,858 --> 00:17:41,362
[Tom hums to soundtrack]

363
00:17:41,428 --> 00:17:43,063
[Joel] Movie Beater Paints.

364
00:17:43,497 --> 00:17:46,167
For bad American movies, like yours.

365
00:17:49,603 --> 00:17:50,903
Ha. Pat Dinga.

366
00:17:51,003 --> 00:17:52,605
He was a Dingaling brother.

367
00:17:55,108 --> 00:17:57,710
[Joel] Oh, they're fictitious.
That's no fair.

368
00:17:58,278 --> 00:17:59,547
[Tom] I don't know.

369
00:18:01,548 --> 00:18:04,350
Mr. Wood's wardrobe by Mrs. Wood.

370
00:18:04,452 --> 00:18:05,485
[laughing]

371
00:18:06,287 --> 00:18:07,922
Oh, my God, Mr. Trevino!

372
00:18:08,122 --> 00:18:09,990
[Tom] This is the worst storm yet!

373
00:18:10,057 --> 00:18:12,025
Yeah, almost every night
for the last three months.

374
00:18:12,125 --> 00:18:13,860
-[Joel] Hey, it's Bert and Ernie.
-Sure ain't natural!

375
00:18:17,597 --> 00:18:19,533
Let's head back to the clubhouse.

376
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:22,468
I'm getting away from this lightning
catcher while I still can!

377
00:18:22,535 --> 00:18:24,905
You'll be soaked to the bone
before you get ten yards.

378
00:18:25,405 --> 00:18:27,673
Well, I'd rather be
soaked than barbequed!

379
00:18:27,740 --> 00:18:29,610
[Tom] I'd like to be lightly braised!

380
00:18:29,677 --> 00:18:32,847
[Joel] Just kiss instead of talking
to him to death.

381
00:18:35,548 --> 00:18:37,985
So, what are we doing out here again?

382
00:18:40,287 --> 00:18:41,622
[Tom] Back here. Ha.

383
00:18:41,688 --> 00:18:43,057
How did that happen?

384
00:18:43,657 --> 00:18:46,527
What if we ran for it?

385
00:18:46,593 --> 00:18:48,928
It's impossible to make it
to the main road.

386
00:18:48,995 --> 00:18:51,465
Maybe we can make it
to the old Willow's place.

387
00:18:51,532 --> 00:18:52,732
Are you out of your mind?

388
00:18:52,798 --> 00:18:54,935
[Joel] No, but thanks for asking.

389
00:18:56,303 --> 00:18:57,803
The newspaper stories say--

390
00:18:57,872 --> 00:18:59,707
Oh, act your age, man!

391
00:19:03,377 --> 00:19:06,045
[Tom] What the-- Son of a gun,
we're back here again.

392
00:19:06,112 --> 00:19:07,413
How'd we do that?

393
00:19:08,548 --> 00:19:10,117
Don't look good to me.

394
00:19:10,183 --> 00:19:11,485
Ah, you're just spooked.

395
00:19:11,718 --> 00:19:14,588
Spooked enough.
That place just don't look healthy.

396
00:19:14,655 --> 00:19:16,923
It hasn't been lived in in 15 years.

397
00:19:16,990 --> 00:19:17,958
It's deserted.

398
00:19:18,025 --> 00:19:19,058
It is, huh?

399
00:19:19,125 --> 00:19:21,495
I still want to have a picnic, damn it.

400
00:19:24,932 --> 00:19:27,067
[Joel] Hey, it's Boo Radley's house.

401
00:19:27,133 --> 00:19:28,302
You said this place was deserted.

402
00:19:28,368 --> 00:19:30,237
Well, it's supposed to be.
I don't get it.

403
00:19:31,738 --> 00:19:32,973
[Tom] Hi, it's Curt Gowdy.

404
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:34,875
Can me and Phil Harris come in?

405
00:19:34,942 --> 00:19:37,710
No, I don't want a Chevrolet.

406
00:19:39,345 --> 00:19:40,347
What do you want?

407
00:19:40,413 --> 00:19:43,150
[Tom] Oh, your old hip shipment's in,
Mr. Lugosi, sir.

408
00:19:43,217 --> 00:19:44,818
I didn't think anybody lived here.

409
00:19:45,085 --> 00:19:46,220
So, here's an idea.

410
00:19:46,453 --> 00:19:48,422
We thought maybe
we could get out of the rain.

411
00:19:48,488 --> 00:19:50,490
Looks like it's going to last all night.

412
00:19:50,523 --> 00:19:51,858
Yeah, it's pretty bad out there.

413
00:19:51,925 --> 00:19:54,562
-Oh, no. You can't stay.
-[Joel] Thanks for coming by, though.

414
00:19:54,628 --> 00:19:56,230
You can't stay.

415
00:19:56,297 --> 00:19:57,763
We might catch pneumonia out there.

416
00:19:57,830 --> 00:19:59,933
You're not welcome in my house.

417
00:20:00,033 --> 00:20:01,468
Go away. Now.

418
00:20:01,535 --> 00:20:04,905
[Crow] Besides, pneumonia is a virus
and can't be caught just by cold weather.

419
00:20:05,472 --> 00:20:08,775
-Go, go.
- [Joel] Oh, cut! Tor, you're not in yet!

420
00:20:08,842 --> 00:20:09,943
-A monster!
-[Tom] Try again.

421
00:20:10,010 --> 00:20:11,878
Now you believe those newspaper stories?

422
00:20:11,945 --> 00:20:15,315
You hear that, Lobo?
You're the monster.

423
00:20:15,382 --> 00:20:18,052
Perhaps one day you will meet the monster.

424
00:20:18,118 --> 00:20:20,487
Tor, close your mouth, you'll drown!

425
00:20:22,155 --> 00:20:23,690
[Joel] Nice guys.

426
00:20:25,025 --> 00:20:26,593
[Tom] Now, back to my novel.

427
00:20:26,660 --> 00:20:27,793
Where was I?

428
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:36,437
Oh, that's a lovely pantsuit.

429
00:20:36,503 --> 00:20:39,773
[Joel] Ahh, time for be in movie?

430
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:41,908
[Tom] Wipe your feet, Tor.
Come on.

431
00:20:46,012 --> 00:20:48,182
I wish I didn't have a roommate.

432
00:20:48,415 --> 00:20:49,448
Well...

433
00:20:50,283 --> 00:20:52,585
[Joel] And stay out of my Ford, Tor.

434
00:20:53,687 --> 00:20:55,455
[Tom] Honey, I'm home.

435
00:20:57,923 --> 00:20:59,893
I'm just going to have
a Slim Fast and relax.

436
00:21:02,228 --> 00:21:04,698
[Tom]
♪ It's a sinister day in the laboratory ♪

437
00:21:04,765 --> 00:21:06,667
♪ A sinister day in the lab ♪

438
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:07,800
Hello.

439
00:21:12,138 --> 00:21:14,073
[Joel] Hey, it's Dr. Clayton Forester.

440
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:16,175
-[all laugh]
-What a jerk.

441
00:21:18,078 --> 00:21:19,980
[Tom] Ah, my first appointment is here.

442
00:21:23,617 --> 00:21:25,585
Well, I got to slap out
the tank pretty soon.

443
00:21:25,652 --> 00:21:27,420
Filled with octo-poopy.

444
00:21:28,655 --> 00:21:31,325
[Tom] Hey, he's got the 80s
corporate look for women.

445
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:35,062
[Joel] I love how I do that.

446
00:21:35,128 --> 00:21:36,128
I do.

447
00:21:38,165 --> 00:21:40,833
He's so coy.

448
00:21:43,703 --> 00:21:46,172
[Joel] And he does facials and waxings,
I hear.

449
00:21:47,940 --> 00:21:49,977
I'll take that one.

450
00:21:51,310 --> 00:21:54,815
[Tom, as if underwater] Hey, Dad.
Dad, watch me. Look what I can do.

451
00:21:55,682 --> 00:21:58,452
[Joel] Oh, I have an octopus.
Hmm?

452
00:22:02,022 --> 00:22:03,923
Aww, he's sick.

453
00:22:05,958 --> 00:22:08,195
[Tom] He's writing a message, here.

454
00:22:08,262 --> 00:22:09,628
Clean this tank.

455
00:22:13,667 --> 00:22:15,968
Hey, where the hell
do we live again, Steve?

456
00:22:16,035 --> 00:22:17,403
Wait, wait! I can't run anymore.

457
00:22:17,470 --> 00:22:18,572
[Tom] I don't know?

458
00:22:19,105 --> 00:22:20,507
We've got to keep going.

459
00:22:20,573 --> 00:22:22,842
That-- that was the monster.

460
00:22:22,908 --> 00:22:25,812
What I wouldn't give to see
a couple of cops right about now.

461
00:22:25,878 --> 00:22:26,880
Come on.

462
00:22:28,013 --> 00:22:29,282
[Tom burbles as if underwater]

463
00:22:29,983 --> 00:22:33,053
[Joel] Ah. Just a sec, I broke a heel.

464
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:35,155
-[Tom] I poofed.
-[man] Ahhh!

465
00:22:37,257 --> 00:22:38,558
[Tom] You say something, Roy?

466
00:22:40,027 --> 00:22:42,162
[Joel] I got water in my galoshes.

467
00:22:42,228 --> 00:22:43,263
Ha-- Ow.

468
00:22:43,630 --> 00:22:44,630
Quit playing around.

469
00:22:45,498 --> 00:22:48,835
[Tom] Uh, folks.
Try to link these two shots together.

470
00:22:48,902 --> 00:22:50,570
-[man continues screaming]
-[Crow laughing]

471
00:22:50,637 --> 00:22:53,373
[Joel] I don't know
what he's screaming about, really.

472
00:22:53,840 --> 00:22:55,275
Help!

473
00:22:55,375 --> 00:22:58,812
I'm doing the best I can, Roy,
the screaming doesn't really help me.

474
00:23:01,147 --> 00:23:03,048
[Tom] Well, my shift's over.
I've got to go.

475
00:23:03,117 --> 00:23:04,818
[screaming continues]

476
00:23:04,918 --> 00:23:07,187
[Joel] Did you ever hear of friendly fire?

477
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:09,088
[screaming]

478
00:23:09,155 --> 00:23:10,923
Embrace death, Roy.

479
00:23:11,023 --> 00:23:12,992
-[Tom] He's into it.
-[screaming]

480
00:23:13,058 --> 00:23:14,795
[Tom] It's an eight-point octopi
you've got there.

481
00:23:14,862 --> 00:23:16,897
[screaming]

482
00:23:16,963 --> 00:23:18,063
That's a keeper.

483
00:23:18,132 --> 00:23:20,233
[screaming]

484
00:23:21,668 --> 00:23:23,137
[all] Oh, shut up!

485
00:23:23,203 --> 00:23:26,507
[Joel] You know, I thought being killed by
an octopus was supposed to be peaceful.

486
00:23:26,573 --> 00:23:27,573
[Tom] Nuh-uh.

487
00:23:29,708 --> 00:23:31,778
[Joel] Now, for the other kneecap.

488
00:23:32,578 --> 00:23:33,580
[groaning]

489
00:23:34,613 --> 00:23:36,015
[Joel] Buddy, buddy.

490
00:23:36,082 --> 00:23:38,718
I will love him
and feed him and call him mine.

491
00:23:40,053 --> 00:23:41,988
-[Tom] Time to go.
-[Joel] Time to go.

492
00:23:42,055 --> 00:23:44,357
-[Joel whistles]
-[Tom hums]

493
00:24:00,040 --> 00:24:01,040
[Joel] ♪ He's hired ♪

494
00:24:01,107 --> 00:24:02,908
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ He's hired ♪

495
00:24:02,975 --> 00:24:05,445
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ I'm hired ♪

496
00:24:05,512 --> 00:24:08,882
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ I hope I don't get fired ♪

497
00:24:08,948 --> 00:24:12,285
-♪ He's fired ♪
-♪ In 40 years I'll be retired ♪

498
00:24:12,352 --> 00:24:16,088
♪ But for now I'm simply hired ♪

499
00:24:16,222 --> 00:24:17,623
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ He's hired ♪

500
00:24:17,723 --> 00:24:18,825
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ He's hired ♪

501
00:24:18,858 --> 00:24:22,095
-♪ I got a job today ♪
-♪ He's hired ♪

502
00:24:22,162 --> 00:24:24,932
♪ I'm selling Chevrolets ♪

503
00:24:24,998 --> 00:24:28,735
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ I'm bringing home good pay ♪

504
00:24:28,802 --> 00:24:32,172
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ I just got hired ♪

505
00:24:32,238 --> 00:24:33,707
-♪ He's hired ♪
-♪ He's hired ♪

506
00:24:33,773 --> 00:24:34,875
♪ He's hired ♪

507
00:24:34,942 --> 00:24:37,777
-♪ He got a job today ♪
-♪ Yeah, me ♪

508
00:24:37,843 --> 00:24:41,582
-♪ He's selling Chevrolets ♪
-♪ We're going to get holidays ♪

509
00:24:41,648 --> 00:24:45,252
-♪ He's bringing home good pay ♪
-♪ Maybe even dessert ♪

510
00:24:45,318 --> 00:24:49,122
-♪ He just got hired ♪
-♪ I just got hired ♪

511
00:24:50,590 --> 00:24:52,025
♪ Hired ♪

512
00:24:53,260 --> 00:24:55,728
♪ I was wondering
If you'd like to see my-- ♪

513
00:24:55,795 --> 00:24:56,930
No!

514
00:24:56,997 --> 00:24:59,132
♪ But I really think you should see my-- ♪

515
00:24:59,198 --> 00:25:00,200
No!

516
00:25:00,967 --> 00:25:02,435
-♪ It's got a lovely-- ♪
-No!

517
00:25:02,502 --> 00:25:04,537
-♪ And if only you would-- ♪
-No!

518
00:25:04,603 --> 00:25:07,473
♪ Can't you see
We're trying to tell you no? ♪

519
00:25:10,543 --> 00:25:12,512
[Tom sighs heavily]

520
00:25:13,480 --> 00:25:17,317
♪ Zeroes ♪

521
00:25:17,850 --> 00:25:21,855
♪ Zeroes ♪

522
00:25:21,922 --> 00:25:27,893
♪ None of my salesmen
Has ever sold a car ♪

523
00:25:29,028 --> 00:25:35,035
♪ Sometimes I think
I might be pushing them too far ♪

524
00:25:36,068 --> 00:25:39,405
♪ I think I'll hit the bar ♪

525
00:25:39,538 --> 00:25:44,912
♪ And try to get my mind off zeroes ♪

526
00:25:45,478 --> 00:25:47,213
[Gypsy] ♪ Zeroes ♪

527
00:25:47,280 --> 00:25:48,782
♪ Zeroes ♪

528
00:25:49,482 --> 00:25:51,250
[Gypsy] ♪ Zeroes ♪

529
00:25:51,317 --> 00:25:56,823
♪ All of my salesmen are ♪

530
00:25:57,023 --> 00:26:00,693
♪ Zeroes ♪

531
00:26:01,460 --> 00:26:02,462
[sighs]

532
00:26:07,033 --> 00:26:09,435
♪ Good evening, Sonny ♪

533
00:26:09,502 --> 00:26:11,638
♪ Hi, Dad ♪

534
00:26:11,705 --> 00:26:13,507
♪ You look depressed ♪

535
00:26:13,573 --> 00:26:15,408
♪ I am ♪

536
00:26:15,475 --> 00:26:18,043
♪ Come on and have some lemonade ♪

537
00:26:18,110 --> 00:26:21,013
♪ Thanks, Mom, but that won't help ♪

538
00:26:21,080 --> 00:26:23,883
♪ Just get it off your chest ♪

539
00:26:24,017 --> 00:26:27,520
♪ ok ♪

540
00:26:29,788 --> 00:26:31,523
♪ I suck at my job ♪

541
00:26:31,625 --> 00:26:32,858
-♪ No, you don't ♪
-♪ Yes, I do ♪

542
00:26:32,925 --> 00:26:33,927
Oh, my.

543
00:26:33,993 --> 00:26:36,062
-♪ My salesmen are slobs ♪
-♪ No, they aren't ♪

544
00:26:36,128 --> 00:26:38,230
-♪ Yes, they do ♪
-Huh?

545
00:26:38,298 --> 00:26:41,768
♪ I'd like to make a sale
But what can I do? ♪

546
00:26:41,835 --> 00:26:43,370
♪ I'm going to be a failure ♪

547
00:26:43,437 --> 00:26:45,938
-♪ Just like you, Dad ♪
-Like me?

548
00:26:46,005 --> 00:26:48,842
♪ That's right
I'll be a failure just like you ♪

549
00:26:50,043 --> 00:26:52,078
[mumbling]

550
00:26:52,145 --> 00:26:55,015
Extry, extry, read all about it.

551
00:26:55,082 --> 00:26:56,850
Pearl Harbor bombed.

552
00:26:56,917 --> 00:26:58,885
Roosevelt declares war.

553
00:26:58,952 --> 00:27:00,453
-Well, that's it.
-What's it?

554
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:01,955
Uncle Sam is going to need cars.

555
00:27:02,022 --> 00:27:05,358
Why, selling Chevrolets is going to help
the war effort, don't you see that?

556
00:27:05,425 --> 00:27:06,592
Come on, everybody.

557
00:27:06,660 --> 00:27:12,732
♪ See the USA ♪

558
00:27:12,832 --> 00:27:14,767
♪ In a Chevro-- ♪

559
00:27:14,900 --> 00:27:17,503
-We got commercials time.
-For crying out loud.

560
00:27:17,570 --> 00:27:19,005
What's the point?

561
00:27:19,072 --> 00:27:20,207
Commercial time.

562
00:27:26,813 --> 00:27:27,813
Who are you?

563
00:27:28,882 --> 00:27:31,317
-Dr. Eric Vornoff.
-[Tom] Walmart?

564
00:27:31,450 --> 00:27:33,920
The name will mean little to you.

565
00:27:37,323 --> 00:27:39,692
What, like they're going
to show a film on his uvula?

566
00:27:43,863 --> 00:27:45,232
[Joel] Uh, gimlet?

567
00:27:46,532 --> 00:27:48,935
[Tom] Employees must wash hands
before returning to work.

568
00:27:51,570 --> 00:27:53,440
He's filled with parabolas.

569
00:27:56,442 --> 00:27:58,277
[Joel] Oh, nice carotid.

570
00:27:58,378 --> 00:28:01,647
[Tom] OK, now, did you give
your family history to Tor?

571
00:28:02,682 --> 00:28:05,685
[Joel] Uh, just let me microwave
my burrito, here.

572
00:28:06,418 --> 00:28:08,788
[Tom] Well, I see by your records,
you haven't been here for six years.

573
00:28:08,855 --> 00:28:10,123
Have you been flossing?

574
00:28:12,892 --> 00:28:15,328
Heh-heh-heh.
Couple of these, couple of these.

575
00:28:17,997 --> 00:28:19,265
What are you doing to me?

576
00:28:19,332 --> 00:28:21,300
[Joel] You're going to be
Myra Breckenridge.

577
00:28:21,367 --> 00:28:23,035
As big as a giant--

578
00:28:23,102 --> 00:28:25,372
But it's not covered by his insurance.

579
00:28:25,472 --> 00:28:28,408
With the strength of 20 men.

580
00:28:28,475 --> 00:28:29,508
[Joel] Give or take a few.

581
00:28:29,575 --> 00:28:31,543
-Or--
-[Tom] Not.

582
00:28:31,610 --> 00:28:34,780
Like all the others... dead.

583
00:28:34,847 --> 00:28:36,215
[screaming]

584
00:28:36,883 --> 00:28:37,883
Oops.

585
00:28:42,388 --> 00:28:43,388
[Joel] What?

586
00:28:43,457 --> 00:28:45,892
So much for the giant theory.

587
00:28:50,028 --> 00:28:51,097
Turn off the switch.

588
00:28:51,163 --> 00:28:52,865
[Tom] And get your lawyer on the phone.

589
00:28:54,233 --> 00:28:57,037
[Joel] Hmm.
He doesn't seem that much gianter.

590
00:28:58,938 --> 00:29:00,973
I can barely hear his heart beating.

591
00:29:01,673 --> 00:29:03,710
[Tom] I can't help this man, he's dead.

592
00:29:05,112 --> 00:29:07,747
Uh, boss, anything I can do?

593
00:29:09,515 --> 00:29:12,618
[Joel] Uh, Lobo, where am I
supposed to take a pulse?

594
00:29:13,018 --> 00:29:15,020
[Tom] Well, so much
for the grant money, Bela.

595
00:29:16,957 --> 00:29:19,658
Tor, your internship
has taken a downturn.

596
00:29:21,127 --> 00:29:24,830
[Joel] Uh, Tor's heart go out to doctor.

597
00:29:24,897 --> 00:29:27,233
[Tom] You win some, you lose some, eh?

598
00:29:28,902 --> 00:29:30,970
Well, throw him on the heap, Tor.

599
00:29:32,305 --> 00:29:35,775
[Tom] Tonight, on Dr. Kevorkian, MD.

600
00:29:35,975 --> 00:29:38,343
[Joel] I'm going to go get a Yoplait.

601
00:29:40,713 --> 00:29:42,448
Isn't it strange, Lobo?

602
00:29:42,515 --> 00:29:43,950
[Joel] What's that, boss?

603
00:29:44,183 --> 00:29:47,387
Our friend always returns home

604
00:29:47,453 --> 00:29:50,523
after his long, tiresome swim.

605
00:29:50,957 --> 00:29:53,727
I just hate it
when Bela waxes philosophical.

606
00:29:54,660 --> 00:29:56,362
[Joel] Makes you think, don't it?

607
00:29:56,428 --> 00:29:58,363
[Tom] Oh?

608
00:29:58,430 --> 00:30:00,567
[Tom] Ahh, new tax bill may be needed.

609
00:30:01,533 --> 00:30:03,870
Hey, look, building code is under fire.

610
00:30:04,670 --> 00:30:06,873
[Joel] It's An American in Cleveland.

611
00:30:07,573 --> 00:30:09,742
[Tom] It's Walk, Don't Run.

612
00:30:11,210 --> 00:30:14,147
[Tom hums to soundtrack]

613
00:30:16,348 --> 00:30:17,583
Huh?

614
00:30:18,852 --> 00:30:21,620
Well, Mr. Belushi, I think
you'll like the Chateau Marmont.

615
00:30:24,357 --> 00:30:25,492
[Tom] Columbo.

616
00:30:25,958 --> 00:30:27,293
-What's your name?
- [Joel] Who's your daddy?

617
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:29,462
-You find out.
-We will.

618
00:30:29,528 --> 00:30:31,063
Hey, watch that cigarette.

619
00:30:31,130 --> 00:30:33,132
-[all] Norm!
-Whoo!

620
00:30:33,232 --> 00:30:35,568
-What were you doing in the swamp?
-What's it to you?

621
00:30:35,635 --> 00:30:36,668
That's enough of that, buster.

622
00:30:37,137 --> 00:30:38,370
Hello, there.

623
00:30:38,437 --> 00:30:41,073
No tank town jail can hold me.

624
00:30:41,140 --> 00:30:43,408
I'll be out of this here
rat trap in 24 hours.

625
00:30:43,508 --> 00:30:45,212
That's what you think.

626
00:30:45,945 --> 00:30:48,247
Vagrancy will hold you for 72 hours.

627
00:30:48,347 --> 00:30:49,648
Take him downstairs and book him.

628
00:30:49,748 --> 00:30:52,618
-All right, let's go.
-[Tom] It's Jackie Gleason as Zhigo.

629
00:30:52,685 --> 00:30:55,288
-You can't do that to me.
-Let's go.

630
00:30:57,623 --> 00:31:00,527
Hello, there!

631
00:31:00,593 --> 00:31:02,828
[Joel] That was Marty Allen.

632
00:31:04,630 --> 00:31:06,398
-[Tom] Hey, little weird guy.
-Hey.

633
00:31:06,465 --> 00:31:07,700
Where do you think you're going?

634
00:31:07,800 --> 00:31:09,568
-[all] Whitey!
-Where do I think I'm going?

635
00:31:09,635 --> 00:31:11,470
I've got Captain Robbins' newspapers.

636
00:31:11,537 --> 00:31:12,672
You have, have you?

637
00:31:12,738 --> 00:31:14,707
And since when you don't leave
the captain's papers with me?

638
00:31:14,773 --> 00:31:16,875
Since the captain told me
to bring them in myself.

639
00:31:16,943 --> 00:31:18,477
When you read them first,
you mess them up.

640
00:31:18,578 --> 00:31:20,178
Hold your tongue and
give me those papers.

641
00:31:20,247 --> 00:31:22,548
I'll have you locked up
for peddling without a license.

642
00:31:22,615 --> 00:31:24,217
Kelton, I've got a license.

643
00:31:24,617 --> 00:31:26,585
Wanna bet I could make it expire
all of a sudden?

644
00:31:26,652 --> 00:31:29,122
Johnny Carson, in a role
that will surprise you.

645
00:31:30,490 --> 00:31:33,325
[Tom] I'm going to get into a suit
and get on the Superman show,

646
00:31:33,392 --> 00:31:35,428
and show you a thing or two.

647
00:31:35,462 --> 00:31:38,530
[Joel] Yeah, I get to read Zaslow first.

648
00:31:38,598 --> 00:31:39,898
There.

649
00:31:39,965 --> 00:31:41,233
Ooh. Dang.

650
00:31:41,367 --> 00:31:43,535
Act natural. Act natural.

651
00:31:43,603 --> 00:31:44,637
Doo-doo-doo.

652
00:31:46,638 --> 00:31:48,842
[Tom] Ah, Mr. Homicide.

653
00:31:52,478 --> 00:31:53,780
Here's the late editions, Captain.

654
00:31:53,847 --> 00:31:55,915
- [Joel] Robert Ludlum?
-Look, why can't I work on this case?

655
00:31:55,982 --> 00:31:59,085
-Go back to your desk.
-Yes, sir.

656
00:31:59,652 --> 00:32:01,320
-And Kelton.
-Yes, sir?

657
00:32:01,387 --> 00:32:02,388
Kiss the birdie.

658
00:32:02,455 --> 00:32:03,488
I told the boy to bring
the papers in himself.

659
00:32:04,123 --> 00:32:05,692
Yes, sir. But if you ask me--

660
00:32:05,758 --> 00:32:08,327
-I didn't.
-Yes, sir.

661
00:32:08,393 --> 00:32:09,828
-And Kelton?
-Yes, sir.

662
00:32:10,430 --> 00:32:11,530
Where's Lieutenant Craig?

663
00:32:11,965 --> 00:32:13,032
In his office, I think.

664
00:32:13,132 --> 00:32:14,700
-Send him in.
-Yes, sir.

665
00:32:14,767 --> 00:32:15,868
Right away, sir.

666
00:32:15,935 --> 00:32:17,970
[Tom] And get me my index finger.

667
00:32:19,605 --> 00:32:22,842
[Joel] Wait a minute.
There's bird poopy on my paper.

668
00:32:23,475 --> 00:32:25,010
Oh, let me see here.

669
00:32:25,077 --> 00:32:28,147
Ed Wood caught wearing
a dress? Again?

670
00:32:28,380 --> 00:32:29,682
[Tom humming]

671
00:32:29,748 --> 00:32:31,750
I just don't get that Gary Larson.

672
00:32:33,285 --> 00:32:35,120
[Joel] Oh, I'm beat.

673
00:32:38,123 --> 00:32:43,628
[Tom humming]

674
00:32:43,663 --> 00:32:47,600
No one will be seated
during the gripping water cooler scene.

675
00:32:47,667 --> 00:32:51,703
[Joel] You know, installing this
Rumplemintz cooler was a great idea.

676
00:32:56,075 --> 00:32:58,210
[all] Finish it. Finish it!

677
00:32:58,277 --> 00:32:59,778
Come on!

678
00:33:00,547 --> 00:33:01,613
Hi, Bavara.

679
00:33:01,680 --> 00:33:03,382
Hello, Dick. Sit down.

680
00:33:03,448 --> 00:33:05,283
[Joel] Now, say hello to the bird.

681
00:33:05,352 --> 00:33:06,852
Is there anyone who hasn't?

682
00:33:06,918 --> 00:33:08,320
[Tom] You can say that again.

683
00:33:08,387 --> 00:33:10,390
There's one nice thing about birds.

684
00:33:10,457 --> 00:33:12,058
They never cause anybody any trouble.

685
00:33:12,125 --> 00:33:15,093
-Yeah, tell that to Tippy Hedren.
-Your girlfriend writes a good story.

686
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:16,228
She thinks so.

687
00:33:16,262 --> 00:33:17,597
-Anything new?
-No.

688
00:33:17,663 --> 00:33:18,663
[Joel] You?

689
00:33:18,732 --> 00:33:21,900
Twelve disappearances around this place
and nothing to go on.

690
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:23,368
And nothing this time, either.

691
00:33:23,770 --> 00:33:27,673
I've been around that swamp so many times,
I'm getting web-footed.

692
00:33:27,740 --> 00:33:29,142
[Tom] Well, don't kill me over it.

693
00:33:29,942 --> 00:33:33,245
-It's been fired recently.
-It's been fired several times recently.

694
00:33:33,312 --> 00:33:34,880
Taking target practice on the bird.

695
00:33:34,947 --> 00:33:36,448
Identified it a little while ago.

696
00:33:37,283 --> 00:33:39,918
The coat belonged to a Leif McCray,
his partner.

697
00:33:39,985 --> 00:33:42,722
- [Joel] You want it?
-Went hunting out by Marsh Lake.

698
00:33:42,788 --> 00:33:44,990
-Never returned.
-Not much left of it.

699
00:33:45,057 --> 00:33:46,858
The boys found it floating in the lake.

700
00:33:46,958 --> 00:33:48,293
What about the rifle?

701
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:50,228
About a mile from the old Willow place.

702
00:33:50,328 --> 00:33:52,965
-All ties together.
-But it doesn't prove anything.

703
00:33:53,032 --> 00:33:55,635
You think there's anything
in these monster stories?

704
00:33:55,702 --> 00:33:56,902
Your girlfriend does.

705
00:33:56,968 --> 00:33:58,403
Leave her out of this.

706
00:33:58,470 --> 00:34:00,172
The police don't believe in monsters.

707
00:34:00,238 --> 00:34:01,507
Facts are our business.

708
00:34:01,573 --> 00:34:02,642
Facts and only facts.

709
00:34:02,708 --> 00:34:03,977
And don't you forget it.

710
00:34:04,077 --> 00:34:06,078
I'm telling you
the captain doesn't see nobody.

711
00:34:06,145 --> 00:34:07,780
[Tom] He'll want to see these.

712
00:34:07,847 --> 00:34:12,050
Captain Robbins, will you tell
this junior G-man to let me go?

713
00:34:12,118 --> 00:34:13,585
-[Robbins] Kelton.
-Yes, sir?

714
00:34:13,685 --> 00:34:17,523
-Let her go.
-[Tom] Ed Wood's cameo appearance here.

715
00:34:18,357 --> 00:34:22,128
It used to be a newspaper reporter
could get information around this place.

716
00:34:22,195 --> 00:34:24,697
- [Joel] For a small fee.
-What in the world do you mean?

717
00:34:24,797 --> 00:34:26,732
Now he makes like a comic.

718
00:34:26,798 --> 00:34:29,468
Dick Craig, I don't put much stock
in the future success

719
00:34:29,535 --> 00:34:32,705
of our married life if already
you're holding out secrets on me.

720
00:34:32,772 --> 00:34:34,840
- [Joel] Hmm?
-You've been dodging me all day.

721
00:34:34,907 --> 00:34:36,708
-Have we met?
-Where'd you get an idea like that?

722
00:34:36,775 --> 00:34:39,412
-You mean you haven't been dodging me?
-[Tom] I was actively avoiding you.

723
00:34:39,478 --> 00:34:42,615
And all those times I called and Kelton
and said the line was busy,

724
00:34:42,682 --> 00:34:44,417
-it really was?
-Yes.

725
00:34:45,017 --> 00:34:48,320
OK. Let's have the story
on Lake Marsh and the monster.

726
00:34:48,553 --> 00:34:51,590
-Ah, monster, monster.
-[Tom] Who's got the monster?

727
00:34:51,657 --> 00:34:53,392
I thought so.

728
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:56,462
There are no such thing as monsters.
This is the 20th century.

729
00:34:56,562 --> 00:34:57,997
Don't count on it.

730
00:34:58,063 --> 00:34:59,132
Monsters, I mean.

731
00:34:59,232 --> 00:35:00,633
-Now, Janet.
- [Joel] Damn it.

732
00:35:00,700 --> 00:35:03,768
Don't you "Now, Janet" me.
And you can count our engagement off.

733
00:35:03,837 --> 00:35:05,505
-You don't mean that.
-Yes, I do.

734
00:35:05,605 --> 00:35:06,738
No, she doesn't.

735
00:35:06,838 --> 00:35:08,407
I most certainly do.

736
00:35:08,507 --> 00:35:10,877
-Well, then give him back the ring.
-What?

737
00:35:11,010 --> 00:35:13,412
-This is the best bit.
-Give him back the ring.

738
00:35:13,478 --> 00:35:15,380
-Now, Chief! I--
-Captain.

739
00:35:15,447 --> 00:35:16,782
Give him back the rock.

740
00:35:16,848 --> 00:35:18,483
It's the only fair thing.

741
00:35:18,583 --> 00:35:22,388
I'd rather throw it in Lake Marsh, first.

742
00:35:22,455 --> 00:35:25,525
Besides, we've got to leave our personal
lives out of our business lives.

743
00:35:25,592 --> 00:35:28,127
-As it should be.
-As I've been saying all along.

744
00:35:28,193 --> 00:35:29,928
-Dog.
-[applause]

745
00:35:29,962 --> 00:35:31,563
[Joel] They used to do this in Vaudeville.

746
00:35:31,630 --> 00:35:34,133
That's your own story.
You wrote it, and you're stuck with it.

747
00:35:34,200 --> 00:35:36,068
There's no such thing as monsters.

748
00:35:36,135 --> 00:35:39,705
[Tom laughs insanely]

749
00:35:39,772 --> 00:35:41,140
That makes twelve in three months.

750
00:35:47,613 --> 00:35:51,183
Well, looks like I've run into a dead end
around here.

751
00:35:51,250 --> 00:35:53,318
[Joel] I think I'll go ruin another movie.

752
00:35:53,385 --> 00:35:55,655
If you boys want to
play a game of secrets,

753
00:35:55,722 --> 00:35:57,423
there's nothing left for me to do but take

754
00:35:57,490 --> 00:35:59,458
a run out to Lake Marsh, myself.

755
00:36:00,025 --> 00:36:01,393
Over my dead body.

756
00:36:02,495 --> 00:36:04,097
That could be arranged.

757
00:36:04,163 --> 00:36:06,065
[Tom] It's Lawrencia Bembenek.

758
00:36:06,132 --> 00:36:07,433
Goodbye.

759
00:36:07,533 --> 00:36:09,802
Say goodbye to the bird, he's hurting.

760
00:36:13,305 --> 00:36:14,973
[Tom] Eh, dames.

761
00:36:15,040 --> 00:36:17,277
She's just crazy enough
to do what she says.

762
00:36:17,343 --> 00:36:19,478
-You know what, Captain?
- [Joel] That's what.

763
00:36:19,545 --> 00:36:20,747
I think you're right.

764
00:36:23,348 --> 00:36:25,517
Let's see, who else can I frighten?

765
00:36:25,918 --> 00:36:28,653
[Joel] And we now return
to The Big, Big Car.

766
00:36:28,720 --> 00:36:30,623
Geez, you could stand up in that thing.

767
00:36:31,123 --> 00:36:33,225
[Tom imitating car engine]

768
00:36:34,227 --> 00:36:39,765
[Joel] Oh, the sign says,
"Space Reserved for Creepy Woman. Hm.

769
00:36:42,468 --> 00:36:44,003
This is Lake Marsh?

770
00:36:44,070 --> 00:36:47,138
[Tom] Well, they paved Lake Marsh
and put up a parking lot.

771
00:36:50,275 --> 00:36:52,077
[Joel] Oh, that's the door.
I wouldn't have--

772
00:36:52,143 --> 00:36:54,913
[Tom] ♪ Self-styling a door by the door ♪

773
00:36:55,013 --> 00:36:56,248
Hi, Tillie.

774
00:36:56,315 --> 00:36:58,683
Hi, Janet. Long time no see.

775
00:36:58,750 --> 00:37:00,587
Tillie, do you remember
a couple of years ago,

776
00:37:00,653 --> 00:37:03,155
in the real estate section,
when the old Willow place

777
00:37:03,222 --> 00:37:05,858
out on Lake Marsh was up for sale--
against back taxes?

778
00:37:05,958 --> 00:37:06,958
[all] Yeah!

779
00:37:07,025 --> 00:37:11,497
Seems to me, that was around
November or December of '48.

780
00:37:11,563 --> 00:37:12,632
Want to see the files?

781
00:37:12,732 --> 00:37:14,067
-Yes.
-Just a minute.

782
00:37:14,300 --> 00:37:17,403
That was right after my face
went over to the dark side.

783
00:37:17,503 --> 00:37:20,138
-Here we are.
- [Joel] Oh, no, that's my brownie recipe.

784
00:37:20,205 --> 00:37:22,408
October to December '48.

785
00:37:22,475 --> 00:37:23,675
In the top file there.

786
00:37:23,742 --> 00:37:25,010
I won't be long.

787
00:37:25,110 --> 00:37:26,545
Take your time.
I ain't going any place.

788
00:37:26,612 --> 00:37:28,480
And neither are they.

789
00:37:28,547 --> 00:37:30,215
It's Wilhelmina Demarest.

790
00:37:31,317 --> 00:37:34,120
[Joel] Huh, what do you know,
building codes under fire.

791
00:37:34,187 --> 00:37:35,755
[Tom] Hey, don't touch that sandwich.

792
00:37:37,890 --> 00:37:41,693
Oh, there's a column in Ann Landers
about older women liking cuddling. Hm.

793
00:37:42,462 --> 00:37:43,928
[Tom] What's she-- Ew.

794
00:37:46,532 --> 00:37:47,667
Find what you wanted, Janet?

795
00:37:47,733 --> 00:37:50,102
Yes, thanks.
Sorry to leave such a mess for you.

796
00:37:50,168 --> 00:37:51,737
Well, that's what I get paid for.

797
00:37:52,003 --> 00:37:54,373
Be a dear, Tillie.
Call the boss--

798
00:37:54,440 --> 00:37:56,242
-Springsteen?
-I'll report in later.

799
00:37:56,308 --> 00:37:58,643
Sure thing.
Find something hot?

800
00:37:58,743 --> 00:38:00,012
Could be.

801
00:38:00,545 --> 00:38:01,713
Oh, and Dick!

802
00:38:01,780 --> 00:38:03,282
[Tom] Oh, yes.

803
00:38:03,348 --> 00:38:04,917
Break my dinner date with him.

804
00:38:04,983 --> 00:38:07,520
Tell him I--
Oh, I have an ulcer or something.

805
00:38:07,587 --> 00:38:09,988
Leave it to me. I fix.

806
00:38:10,055 --> 00:38:12,458
What's it like outside?

807
00:38:12,525 --> 00:38:14,627
-Hi, Janet.
-Oh, hi, Marge.

808
00:38:14,693 --> 00:38:17,463
-Janet. Still on the monster hunt?
-What do you think?

809
00:38:17,530 --> 00:38:20,032
I think the boss has been
looking for you all day.

810
00:38:20,132 --> 00:38:23,668
Something about the police
wanting those monster stories 86'ed.

811
00:38:23,735 --> 00:38:25,705
You've got the whole town in a panic.

812
00:38:25,772 --> 00:38:27,038
I didn't hear you.

813
00:38:27,138 --> 00:38:28,973
-I said--
-I know what you said.

814
00:38:29,042 --> 00:38:30,208
But I didn't hear you.

815
00:38:30,308 --> 00:38:32,210
-Big face theater.
-I get it.

816
00:38:32,277 --> 00:38:35,682
-See you later.
-Dun de da da da-- Ahh!

817
00:38:37,315 --> 00:38:39,318
[Tom imitating birds chirping]

818
00:38:42,988 --> 00:38:45,423
[Joel] Hey, scooch over, Mr. Hoffa.

819
00:38:50,328 --> 00:38:51,630
Now, now, Mrs. Webb, no.

820
00:38:51,697 --> 00:38:52,932
No. Slowly, Mrs. Webb.

821
00:38:52,965 --> 00:38:54,033
Uh, you are backing up.

822
00:38:54,133 --> 00:38:55,533
You're ba-- Mrs. Webb--

823
00:38:56,568 --> 00:38:59,672
Lieutenant Craig has been working
on this case a long time, professor.

824
00:38:59,738 --> 00:39:01,940
I'd like him to hear
what you just told me.

825
00:39:02,007 --> 00:39:03,408
I should be happy to cooperate.

826
00:39:03,475 --> 00:39:04,577
Oh, hello, Dick.

827
00:39:04,643 --> 00:39:06,112
[Joel] Ah, kiss the bird, Dick.

828
00:39:06,178 --> 00:39:09,282
-Professor Vladimir Strowski,
-[Tom] Yeah, right.

829
00:39:09,348 --> 00:39:11,383
-Glad to meet you, Professor.
-How do you do?

830
00:39:11,717 --> 00:39:13,952
Professor, will you bring him up to date?

831
00:39:14,018 --> 00:39:16,655
[Crow, German accent] Well, I was born
in Krankenheim. My father--

832
00:39:16,955 --> 00:39:18,790
Have you ever heard of "Loch Ness"?

833
00:39:18,857 --> 00:39:20,827
[Joel] Any relation to Elliott Ness?

834
00:39:20,893 --> 00:39:22,260
It's a lake in Scotland, isn't it?

835
00:39:22,327 --> 00:39:24,097
[Tom] No, it's a lake in Scotland.

836
00:39:24,163 --> 00:39:28,133
Then perhaps you are familiar
with the Loch Ness Monster.

837
00:39:28,200 --> 00:39:30,635
[Joel] That's a monster
in a lake in Scotland, right?

838
00:39:30,702 --> 00:39:33,738
A few years ago, was called
in by the British police

839
00:39:33,805 --> 00:39:37,275
to investigate the appearance
of a monster in Loch Ness.

840
00:39:37,342 --> 00:39:42,013
We thought that it was some creature
leftover from a bygone age.

841
00:39:43,015 --> 00:39:45,918
I am considered an authority
on the subject

842
00:39:45,985 --> 00:39:48,353
of prehistoric monsters.

843
00:39:48,420 --> 00:39:50,322
[Joel] He's smelling his thumb.

844
00:39:50,655 --> 00:39:53,558
However, I must admit that
my investigation failed

845
00:39:53,625 --> 00:39:55,560
to get the desired results

846
00:39:55,627 --> 00:39:58,197
in that I did not see the monster myself.

847
00:39:58,263 --> 00:40:00,632
[Tom] Hey, Siegfried, pick an accent
and stick with it, will ya?

848
00:40:00,665 --> 00:40:02,000
There are sworn statements.

849
00:40:02,435 --> 00:40:04,303
-Professor--
-Lunch, yeah.

850
00:40:04,370 --> 00:40:07,005
Is it your theory that
perhaps the Loch Ness Monster

851
00:40:07,072 --> 00:40:09,108
crossed the ocean and came to the swamp?

852
00:40:09,675 --> 00:40:12,812
I consider that possibility
extremely remote.

853
00:40:12,878 --> 00:40:14,447
[Tom] That is the union approach.

854
00:40:14,513 --> 00:40:18,383
The creatures do bear
a certain similarity in that there are

855
00:40:18,450 --> 00:40:22,353
so many varied descriptions
of this so-called monster.

856
00:40:22,455 --> 00:40:24,223
Sausages. Oh, yeah.

857
00:40:24,290 --> 00:40:26,958
I thought perhaps,
with my knowledge of such things,

858
00:40:27,025 --> 00:40:29,128
and with your permission, of course,

859
00:40:29,195 --> 00:40:33,265
that I might be able to shed
some light on this mystery.

860
00:40:33,332 --> 00:40:36,535
[Tom] No, we just need some
light typing and filing.

861
00:40:37,603 --> 00:40:39,338
-What do you think, Dick?
-Denny's?

862
00:40:39,405 --> 00:40:40,405
[Joel] Sounds good.

863
00:40:40,472 --> 00:40:42,908
There's no denying
we need help of some sort.

864
00:40:43,408 --> 00:40:46,078
And you'll keep your findings secret
for the time being?

865
00:40:46,612 --> 00:40:48,047
As you will.

866
00:40:48,147 --> 00:40:50,048
-You don't mind a little company?
-[Tom laughter]

867
00:40:50,115 --> 00:40:52,417
What do you mean, "company"?

868
00:40:52,485 --> 00:40:53,685
[Joel] You know, wrestling!

869
00:40:53,752 --> 00:40:56,355
Lieutenant Craig.
Sometimes the police can be quite useful.

870
00:40:56,422 --> 00:40:58,657
-Abuseful.
-I should be most happy

871
00:40:58,723 --> 00:41:01,327
to have Lieutenant Craig's assistance.

872
00:41:01,393 --> 00:41:04,530
How soon can you be ready to leave
for Lake Marsh, Lieutenant Craig?

873
00:41:04,663 --> 00:41:07,065
-I'm ready.
- [Joel] Should take some time to-- Oh.

874
00:41:07,132 --> 00:41:09,235
I suggest we wait until morning.

875
00:41:09,502 --> 00:41:11,637
There was a storm brewing when I came in.

876
00:41:11,703 --> 00:41:12,738
[Tom] That was the coffee.

877
00:41:12,805 --> 00:41:14,773
So close to nightfall.

878
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:19,445
According to reports,
the monster strikes only at night.

879
00:41:19,512 --> 00:41:21,847
-[Tom] Hee-hee. Funny.
-Undoubtedly true.

880
00:41:21,913 --> 00:41:24,783
-But the preliminary investigation...
-[Tom] Pulmonary?

881
00:41:24,850 --> 00:41:26,518
...should take place at daylight.

882
00:41:26,618 --> 00:41:28,187
The night. The monster.

883
00:41:28,253 --> 00:41:30,188
-And Val.
-All in good time.

884
00:41:31,790 --> 00:41:33,792
-Sounds logical.
-Whatever you say.

885
00:41:33,858 --> 00:41:34,860
[Joel] Not it.

886
00:41:34,927 --> 00:41:37,997
Now, gentlemen, since I just
arrived in town this afternoon,

887
00:41:38,063 --> 00:41:42,367
if you will excuse me,
I should like to return to my hotel.

888
00:41:42,433 --> 00:41:43,768
Certainly, sir.

889
00:41:44,035 --> 00:41:46,072
-Good day, Captain.
-Professor.

890
00:41:46,138 --> 00:41:49,308
Professor, kiss the bird. Kiss it!

891
00:41:49,375 --> 00:41:51,010
[Joel] Oh, uh, could you
recommend a discotheque?

892
00:41:51,077 --> 00:41:53,612
Until morning, Lieutenant Craig.

893
00:41:53,945 --> 00:41:55,413
I'll be here.

894
00:41:55,513 --> 00:41:57,950
[Tom] Whatever. See ya. Buh-bye, now.

895
00:41:59,485 --> 00:42:01,953
[Joel] Say, how did it go in there.
I was just wondering how--

896
00:42:03,188 --> 00:42:04,390
Strange sort of bird.

897
00:42:04,457 --> 00:42:06,058
Don't talk about my bird.

898
00:42:06,692 --> 00:42:07,693
Where do I meet him?

899
00:42:08,293 --> 00:42:11,263
Here, 10:00.
That'll give him plenty of daylight.

900
00:42:11,330 --> 00:42:12,330
[Tom laughs] What?

901
00:42:12,397 --> 00:42:14,567
-Well, see you later.
-Hmm, got a date?

902
00:42:15,167 --> 00:42:17,068
-Why, sir, I'd love--
-What do you think?

903
00:42:17,468 --> 00:42:18,937
I'll say you don't.

904
00:42:19,572 --> 00:42:21,007
-I don't?
- [Joel] Your gun's showing.

905
00:42:21,073 --> 00:42:22,307
Her office called in.

906
00:42:22,373 --> 00:42:26,478
Said that Janet had a headache
or some such thing.

907
00:42:26,578 --> 00:42:28,280
If she's gone to Lake Marsh alone,

908
00:42:28,347 --> 00:42:31,217
I'll take her across my knee
if it's last thing I do.

909
00:42:31,283 --> 00:42:33,852
[Tom] I hope she did.
I hope she did.

910
00:42:33,918 --> 00:42:36,322
-Watch him, Dick.
-Strowski?

911
00:42:36,422 --> 00:42:38,557
-Just a hunch.
-Right.

912
00:42:40,058 --> 00:42:42,828
[Joel] Say, Dick, how did it go in there,
I was just curious--

913
00:42:42,895 --> 00:42:45,163
Nobody's kissing the bird today.

914
00:42:46,032 --> 00:42:49,635
[Joel] Wow, it looks like Anjelica Huston
in The Grifters.

915
00:42:49,735 --> 00:42:51,137
[Tom humming]

916
00:42:53,572 --> 00:42:55,107
[thunder rumbling]

917
00:42:55,840 --> 00:42:58,343
When God was in black and white.

918
00:42:59,678 --> 00:43:02,180
[Joel] Wait a minute.
She skinned a Hostess Snowball,

919
00:43:02,247 --> 00:43:03,715
and now she's wearing it?

920
00:43:04,583 --> 00:43:06,085
[Tom] There, now I'll stay dry.

921
00:43:06,185 --> 00:43:07,953
Coconut works well.

922
00:43:08,353 --> 00:43:11,123
She's weird.
Relatives' ashes in the front yard.

923
00:43:11,190 --> 00:43:12,992
[Joel] Hey, please cover us up, dear.

924
00:43:13,058 --> 00:43:15,860
It's going to rain. Dear?

925
00:43:18,463 --> 00:43:20,365
[Tom] The Senator approaches the bridge.

926
00:43:26,372 --> 00:43:29,140
Let's see.
They said take a left at the elm tree.

927
00:43:29,207 --> 00:43:31,077
Is that an elm? No, that's a--

928
00:43:32,010 --> 00:43:34,245
[Tom] Hee-hee-hee! Wipeout!

929
00:43:36,282 --> 00:43:40,452
[Joel] Uh, uphill, no curve, turn
the wheel to the right, that's right.

930
00:43:40,918 --> 00:43:42,387
Well, it could be worse.

931
00:43:42,453 --> 00:43:43,622
Could be raining.

932
00:43:44,088 --> 00:43:45,090
[Tom] Hmm.

933
00:43:46,525 --> 00:43:49,227
[Joel] Well, big river snake,
better call AAA.

934
00:43:52,463 --> 00:43:55,967
You guys notice how the music
makes it seem more suspenseful?

935
00:43:59,405 --> 00:44:01,707
Meanwhile, a thousand miles away--

936
00:44:03,275 --> 00:44:04,777
-[Tom] Oh, brother.
-[Crow groans]

937
00:44:04,843 --> 00:44:06,045
[Tom] Ed Wood...

938
00:44:06,978 --> 00:44:08,747
[Joel] How do you do it, Ed Wood? Hm.

939
00:44:13,985 --> 00:44:15,420
[screaming]

940
00:44:22,260 --> 00:44:23,362
Ok.

941
00:44:23,428 --> 00:44:24,963
I just really need to say something.

942
00:44:25,030 --> 00:44:26,732
Well, whatever is it, little buddy?

943
00:44:26,798 --> 00:44:29,902
The monster in this experiment
is not scary.

944
00:44:29,968 --> 00:44:32,037
There I said it.
And I'd say it again if I had to.

945
00:44:32,070 --> 00:44:33,872
I'm down with that, Crow.
Here, dig this.

946
00:44:33,938 --> 00:44:37,342
It says here that octopi
rarely even exceed four feet.

947
00:44:37,943 --> 00:44:41,147
Yeah, I know that monster in today's movie
had such a charmed life.

948
00:44:41,213 --> 00:44:44,248
I mean, his victims basically
fell right into his laps.

949
00:44:44,315 --> 00:44:47,720
Yeah, it seems like you can make a monster
out of any sort of sea creature.

950
00:44:47,787 --> 00:44:50,422
You could probably really terrorize
somebody with a flounder.

951
00:44:50,488 --> 00:44:52,223
Of course, you know,
the people back in the Midwest

952
00:44:52,290 --> 00:44:53,858
really didn't know
that much about seafood,

953
00:44:53,925 --> 00:44:55,460
so it probably was pretty spooky.

954
00:44:55,527 --> 00:44:57,830
You know, Ed Wood could have used sushi.

955
00:44:57,897 --> 00:44:58,930
Yeah, or a fish filet.

956
00:44:58,997 --> 00:45:02,167
I mean, sure, it doesn't look that scary,
but once it turns, look out.

957
00:45:02,233 --> 00:45:04,637
Consider the deep sea sandwich
at White Castle.

958
00:45:04,670 --> 00:45:07,272
The name merely implies
that it is a fish product.

959
00:45:07,338 --> 00:45:08,973
Are we getting into processed foods, now?

960
00:45:09,040 --> 00:45:10,708
Sure, as long as they're scary.

961
00:45:10,775 --> 00:45:13,045
-Well, there's olive loaf.
-[all] Blech!

962
00:45:13,112 --> 00:45:14,947
-And there's Dutch loaf.
-[all] Blech!

963
00:45:15,013 --> 00:45:17,817
-Or any kind of loaf.
-[all] Blech!

964
00:45:17,883 --> 00:45:20,452
-Have you ever been to a fish boil?
-[all] Blech!

965
00:45:20,518 --> 00:45:24,055
Or, you know,
once I bit into a cold tater tot.

966
00:45:24,122 --> 00:45:25,557
-[all] Ew!
-Gross me out royally.

967
00:45:25,623 --> 00:45:26,958
You've gone too far, Joel.

968
00:45:27,025 --> 00:45:28,993
You took a fun thing
and made it totally depraved.

969
00:45:29,060 --> 00:45:30,062
Oh, you wussy.

970
00:45:30,128 --> 00:45:33,198
You wouldn't last two minutes with a jar
of dried beef coming at you.

971
00:45:33,265 --> 00:45:36,067
Oh, and, like, what are your odds
going up against a kipper snack?

972
00:45:36,135 --> 00:45:37,568
Hee-hee.

973
00:45:37,635 --> 00:45:38,637
Um...

974
00:45:38,670 --> 00:45:40,205
So, um...

975
00:45:40,405 --> 00:45:41,440
What was the question?

976
00:45:41,507 --> 00:45:46,578
Um, I think he said something
about a flounder something?

977
00:45:46,645 --> 00:45:49,582
Um, didn't you say something
about olive loaf?

978
00:45:49,648 --> 00:45:51,150
-No, I didn't.
-Yeah, you did.

979
00:45:51,217 --> 00:45:54,553
You said something like, "Na na na na
olive loaf-- blah blah blah."

980
00:45:54,620 --> 00:45:56,020
Oh.

981
00:45:56,722 --> 00:45:58,023
[all] Huh.

982
00:45:58,090 --> 00:46:00,725
-[alarm buzzing]
-[all] Ahhh! We've got movie sign!

983
00:46:10,568 --> 00:46:12,370
[Tom] Oh, monsters,
that's what we're talking about.

984
00:46:12,437 --> 00:46:15,840
-Bad snake, bad.
-[Joel] Oh.

985
00:46:16,508 --> 00:46:19,778
Oh, oh, Tor love this.

986
00:46:19,845 --> 00:46:22,713
Tor look fetching when go to church.

987
00:46:24,282 --> 00:46:25,817
[Tom] Oh, gross.

988
00:46:25,883 --> 00:46:26,985
Oh, yuck.

989
00:46:27,052 --> 00:46:29,922
Oh, God! No!
Does he have room for her in there?

990
00:46:30,022 --> 00:46:31,157
[Tom] Ooh.

991
00:46:32,057 --> 00:46:35,327
[Joel] Hey, it's the little
one-room ghoulhouse.

992
00:46:35,393 --> 00:46:36,462
[Tom pretends to snore]

993
00:46:36,595 --> 00:46:39,297
[Dr. Vornoff]
You are all right now, my dear.

994
00:46:39,363 --> 00:46:42,033
[Tom] Oh, I wasn't snoring, was I?

995
00:46:42,100 --> 00:46:43,968
[Joel] I went home with you?

996
00:46:44,035 --> 00:46:45,070
Who-- who are you?

997
00:46:45,137 --> 00:46:48,040
I-- I'm Dr. Eric Vornoff.

998
00:46:48,107 --> 00:46:49,440
Yeah, I invented the world. Yeah.

999
00:46:49,508 --> 00:46:51,343
You had a severe shock.

1000
00:46:51,410 --> 00:46:53,745
[Joel] Oh, yeah,
when I woke up and saw you.

1001
00:46:53,812 --> 00:46:55,313
How did I get here?

1002
00:46:55,380 --> 00:46:58,017
-Oh.
-[Tom] Letting the days go by.

1003
00:46:58,083 --> 00:47:00,118
That's not important for the moment.

1004
00:47:00,185 --> 00:47:01,887
Ah, so you don't know.

1005
00:47:01,953 --> 00:47:05,357
-What you need now is rest.
-[Joel] Rust?

1006
00:47:06,325 --> 00:47:08,293
-Rest.
-[Joel] Rust?

1007
00:47:09,828 --> 00:47:13,132
You feel sleepy.

1008
00:47:13,198 --> 00:47:17,202
[Tom] Gary Oldman's Bram Stoker's
Dracula Hands by Francis Ford Coppola.

1009
00:47:17,302 --> 00:47:18,837
-Sleep.
-[all] Yuck!

1010
00:47:20,938 --> 00:47:23,208
For the lovely young lady--

1011
00:47:23,275 --> 00:47:25,977
[Tom] Of Shady Lane.

1012
00:47:26,043 --> 00:47:27,112
Sleep.

1013
00:47:28,813 --> 00:47:31,217
OK, now.
Wake up, you're late for school.

1014
00:47:32,985 --> 00:47:34,418
[Joel] Meanwhile,
back at Gilligan's Island,

1015
00:47:34,485 --> 00:47:36,822
the Howells make a car
out of coconut leaves.

1016
00:47:36,888 --> 00:47:38,890
[Tom] ♪ We can crash in my Nash ♪

1017
00:47:38,957 --> 00:47:41,827
[Joel] You know, they say Honduras
is really nice this time of year.

1018
00:47:42,060 --> 00:47:43,962
[all humming]

1019
00:47:48,700 --> 00:47:50,002
[Crow] Hmm?

1020
00:47:52,870 --> 00:47:54,973
Hey, I'm following me.

1021
00:47:55,473 --> 00:47:56,875
Well, here we are
at the fork in the road.

1022
00:47:56,942 --> 00:48:00,645
And like they say in all good Westerns,
which way did he go?

1023
00:48:00,712 --> 00:48:02,513
-Well--
-[Tom] Oh, no! He's got a gun!

1024
00:48:02,580 --> 00:48:04,082
That's the road to the Willow's place.

1025
00:48:04,148 --> 00:48:06,818
-Let's take a chance on that one.
-OK.

1026
00:48:07,218 --> 00:48:09,888
You know, I get to hate this swamp
more every time I come out here.

1027
00:48:09,955 --> 00:48:11,957
[Joel] Funny, the swamp likes you.

1028
00:48:12,023 --> 00:48:14,693
-You just weren't born for swamp duty.
-I guess you're right.

1029
00:48:14,760 --> 00:48:17,930
I'd like to transfer to a department
as far away from here as possible.

1030
00:48:17,997 --> 00:48:20,365
Look, Marty, you going to
light these things, or what?

1031
00:48:21,733 --> 00:48:24,468
Gives this place a gleam
that just isn't natural.

1032
00:48:24,535 --> 00:48:26,372
[Tom] Marty, you're dawdling again.

1033
00:48:26,438 --> 00:48:29,942
[Marty] Alive with crawling things.
Crawling death.

1034
00:48:30,208 --> 00:48:31,277
You're right, there, Marty.

1035
00:48:31,543 --> 00:48:32,945
[Joel] Need a vacation, Marty?

1036
00:48:33,012 --> 00:48:34,647
[Dick] This swamp is a monument to death.

1037
00:48:35,380 --> 00:48:40,652
Snakes, alligators, quicksand,
all bent on one thing.

1038
00:48:40,718 --> 00:48:42,520
-[Tom] Kicks
-Destruction.

1039
00:48:42,587 --> 00:48:45,023
-[thunder]
-[Crow hissing]

1040
00:48:45,090 --> 00:48:46,157
[Joel] Ow!

1041
00:48:46,223 --> 00:48:47,960
[Tom] Just you wait.

1042
00:48:48,793 --> 00:48:51,228
Something strange about all this rain.

1043
00:48:51,297 --> 00:48:53,598
-Hey, that rhymes.
-Lightning's been going crazy, too.

1044
00:48:53,665 --> 00:48:56,435
Maybe it's like the papers say,
all these atom bomb explosions

1045
00:48:56,502 --> 00:48:57,970
distorted the atmosphere.

1046
00:48:58,037 --> 00:49:00,272
[Joel] Oh, whoa, whoa.
Don't change the plot, now.

1047
00:49:00,338 --> 00:49:02,073
You know, if I were this
Professor Strowski guy,

1048
00:49:02,140 --> 00:49:04,142
I wouldn't come out in this swap
without a guide.

1049
00:49:04,208 --> 00:49:06,978
You know, that's the one thing
I can't figure.

1050
00:49:07,045 --> 00:49:09,280
-[Tom] Guys.
-He comes to the office.

1051
00:49:09,347 --> 00:49:11,617
[Joel] Oh, ho, ho, I heard this one.

1052
00:49:12,183 --> 00:49:16,422
Makes an appointment to come out here
with us, then goes off by himself.

1053
00:49:16,655 --> 00:49:18,290
Well, are you sure he came out here?

1054
00:49:19,457 --> 00:49:22,193
Ah! Hey!
What do I look like, an ashtray?

1055
00:49:22,727 --> 00:49:24,697
Well, let's get at it.

1056
00:49:24,830 --> 00:49:27,632
[Tom beatboxing to soundtrack]

1057
00:49:27,832 --> 00:49:29,968
[Joel] Boy, that was
an extensive investigation.

1058
00:49:30,035 --> 00:49:31,168
[Tom] Mm-hmm.

1059
00:49:32,903 --> 00:49:34,305
Remember to buckle up, Marty.

1060
00:49:34,438 --> 00:49:35,873
I know it's just a short drive.

1061
00:49:36,908 --> 00:49:38,843
Hands at 10:00 and 2:00, Marty.

1062
00:49:38,910 --> 00:49:40,778
Safety cushion.
Watch your safety cushion.

1063
00:49:40,845 --> 00:49:42,947
Ma-- Mar-- Marty, you're sliding.

1064
00:49:43,015 --> 00:49:45,150
Don't brake. Don't brake. You're sli--

1065
00:49:45,550 --> 00:49:46,552
Oh.

1066
00:49:48,787 --> 00:49:50,055
Blind intersection, Marty!

1067
00:49:50,122 --> 00:49:51,155
Look out, Marty!

1068
00:49:51,222 --> 00:49:52,857
Ma-- When you going to get married, Marty?

1069
00:49:52,923 --> 00:49:54,993
[Joel] Look, you drive.

1070
00:49:56,628 --> 00:49:58,730
[Tom] Hey, look.
Look at this huge car.

1071
00:49:58,797 --> 00:49:59,898
Wonder if it's for sale.

1072
00:49:59,965 --> 00:50:02,300
Yeah, there's not a trace of her.

1073
00:50:02,433 --> 00:50:05,570
Maybe after the accident, she found her
way down to the road and back into town.

1074
00:50:05,637 --> 00:50:06,705
I've got to be sure.

1075
00:50:06,772 --> 00:50:09,340
Look, there's a coffee joint about
ten miles back, let's go and check.

1076
00:50:09,407 --> 00:50:10,642
[Joel] Yeah, I could use a cup.

1077
00:50:10,708 --> 00:50:12,377
Good thinking.
Maybe I get some decaf or something.

1078
00:50:12,443 --> 00:50:14,645
[Tom] Let's have lunch, too.
I love those little sandwiches they make.

1079
00:50:14,713 --> 00:50:17,248
They cut the crusts off,
and they have a great egg salad.

1080
00:50:17,615 --> 00:50:20,052
Uh, guys.
The antenna is stuck in the door again.

1081
00:50:20,118 --> 00:50:21,887
[Tom] Wha--? Oh.

1082
00:50:22,620 --> 00:50:24,255
[Joel] Uh, there she is.

1083
00:50:24,388 --> 00:50:25,423
Oops.

1084
00:50:25,890 --> 00:50:26,892
Blech!

1085
00:50:27,525 --> 00:50:29,127
Defensive driving pays off, Marty.

1086
00:50:29,193 --> 00:50:31,195
That could been you on that hill.

1087
00:50:31,930 --> 00:50:33,532
[Tom] Hey, they changed color and style.

1088
00:50:33,598 --> 00:50:36,668
[Joel] Hey, it's Happy the Wonder Car,
with a built-in juicer. Beep-beep.

1089
00:50:37,535 --> 00:50:38,670
[Tom] Studebaker.

1090
00:50:40,805 --> 00:50:42,873
Coming into the bullpen, Jack McDowell.

1091
00:50:44,910 --> 00:50:47,545
I got it.
The Id, the Ego, and the Superego.

1092
00:50:48,513 --> 00:50:50,948
[Joel] Buck Henry meets Elmer Fudd.

1093
00:50:53,185 --> 00:50:54,552
[Tom] OK, let's see here.

1094
00:50:54,618 --> 00:50:56,988
Scene 12: pull into view, exit car.

1095
00:50:57,055 --> 00:50:58,690
Remember, you're German.

1096
00:50:58,757 --> 00:50:59,858
[Tom] Oh, German. Och.

1097
00:50:59,925 --> 00:51:01,660
Yes. German.
Oh, yes.

1098
00:51:01,760 --> 00:51:04,095
Hello, I'm exiting car, und I'm German.

1099
00:51:04,162 --> 00:51:06,998
German as I could be. Ja. Ja.

1100
00:51:08,333 --> 00:51:09,400
♪ High on the hill-- ♪ Ooh!

1101
00:51:09,467 --> 00:51:11,335
♪ Lived a lonely goatherd-- ♪ Ow!

1102
00:51:11,437 --> 00:51:13,205
♪ Lay dee oh da la-- ♪ Ooh!

1103
00:51:13,272 --> 00:51:14,338
♪ Lay hee hoo ♪

1104
00:51:14,405 --> 00:51:15,407
Ow, ooh.

1105
00:51:16,875 --> 00:51:18,743
White psychiatrist, black heart.

1106
00:51:24,348 --> 00:51:26,485
[Joel] Hey, he found his way to
Wally's filling station.

1107
00:51:26,552 --> 00:51:27,818
[Tom] What do you want on yours?

1108
00:51:27,885 --> 00:51:30,555
Well, we'll get your song right on.
You guys partying down there?

1109
00:51:32,090 --> 00:51:33,358
-Where did you find her car?
-[Tom squawks]

1110
00:51:33,425 --> 00:51:34,993
Yeah, hit him on the car thing.
Good, good, good, that's good.

1111
00:51:35,060 --> 00:51:36,895
-Not a sign of her?
-[Tom] Don't believe that.

1112
00:51:36,962 --> 00:51:38,897
-And what about the car? C'mon.
-Where's Strowski?

1113
00:51:38,963 --> 00:51:40,632
[Tom] Who cares about Strowski?
The girl, the girl.

1114
00:51:40,698 --> 00:51:42,500
I told you not to let him
out of your sight.

1115
00:51:42,633 --> 00:51:44,635
-Fine, Captain.
-[Tom] Give him hell.

1116
00:51:44,702 --> 00:51:46,037
I don't like the looks of things.

1117
00:51:46,070 --> 00:51:47,705
[Tom] Ha! You got him
against the wall, baby. Caw!

1118
00:51:47,772 --> 00:51:49,140
Well, get back to Strowski.

1119
00:51:49,207 --> 00:51:51,643
[Tom] Forget Strowski, give me the phone.
Ohh!

1120
00:51:51,710 --> 00:51:52,977
Yeah.

1121
00:51:53,043 --> 00:51:54,678
He's talking to the bird, Marty.

1122
00:51:54,745 --> 00:51:55,880
You look worried, Dick.

1123
00:51:57,548 --> 00:51:59,283
No one.

1124
00:51:59,350 --> 00:52:02,687
-[Tom] Oh, they're drinking baked beans.
-Captain Robbins, her apartment, office.

1125
00:52:02,753 --> 00:52:04,990
No one has heard from her
since yesterday afternoon.

1126
00:52:05,057 --> 00:52:08,393
-Check the Bates Motel.
-Captain Robbins is checking further.

1127
00:52:08,460 --> 00:52:09,928
OK, but what do we do?

1128
00:52:09,995 --> 00:52:10,995
Orders.

1129
00:52:11,062 --> 00:52:12,297
-Find Strowski.
- [Joel] Boing.

1130
00:52:14,098 --> 00:52:17,135
You know, soup is good food.

1131
00:52:18,068 --> 00:52:20,738
[Tom squawks]
OK, now, call Hertz Mountain.

1132
00:52:20,805 --> 00:52:22,607
Order a gross of seat belts for me.

1133
00:52:22,673 --> 00:52:25,943
Come on, do it now, that's right.
Keep dialing, you chump.

1134
00:52:26,010 --> 00:52:28,513
Oh, uh-oh. Oh.
Oh, oh, I'm going to start tripping.

1135
00:52:28,580 --> 00:52:31,182
Can't help myself. [whistle]
Had a fibrous breakfast.

1136
00:52:31,248 --> 00:52:33,752
Caw. Ah, my vent is opening.
Sorry, oops.

1137
00:52:33,818 --> 00:52:34,920
Oh, oh, let a little go, there.

1138
00:52:34,987 --> 00:52:36,187
-Hello, Jim.
-Hello, Jim.

1139
00:52:36,253 --> 00:52:39,357
-Yeah, about the Lawton girl.
-The Lawton girl?

1140
00:52:39,423 --> 00:52:40,525
He just called me, too.

1141
00:52:40,592 --> 00:52:41,593
He just called me too.

1142
00:52:41,660 --> 00:52:43,328
Who saw her last around your office?

1143
00:52:43,395 --> 00:52:44,997
[Joel] Hmm. Ask him what he's wearing.

1144
00:52:45,063 --> 00:52:46,130
[squawks] Hmm?

1145
00:52:46,665 --> 00:52:50,402
[Tom] Then, when I was eight,
I found that I-- Doctor?

1146
00:52:50,468 --> 00:52:52,070
Doctor.

1147
00:52:54,438 --> 00:52:56,842
Your free continental breakfast.

1148
00:52:57,508 --> 00:53:01,078
[Joel] This is Motel 666.
You get it?

1149
00:53:01,145 --> 00:53:03,047
Don't be afraid of Lobo.

1150
00:53:03,682 --> 00:53:05,183
He's as gentle as a kitten.

1151
00:53:05,250 --> 00:53:08,587
[Tom] A big, bald, deranged kitten
that could snap your neck like celery.

1152
00:53:10,888 --> 00:53:13,123
Me Lobo. Me a waiter.

1153
00:53:13,625 --> 00:53:15,560
That is all. Get out.

1154
00:53:15,627 --> 00:53:17,862
[Joel] Uh, we have other tables, Lobo.

1155
00:53:17,928 --> 00:53:19,330
[Tom] My tip.

1156
00:53:19,397 --> 00:53:22,633
[Joel] Take a picture,
it lasts longer. Geez.

1157
00:53:22,700 --> 00:53:25,637
-I'm so sorry, he's from Barcelona.
-I said get out.

1158
00:53:27,905 --> 00:53:29,473
-[screaming]
-I love these theme restaurants.

1159
00:53:29,540 --> 00:53:31,542
[Tom] This is Madonna's favorite bistro.

1160
00:53:31,610 --> 00:53:33,512
[crying out in pain]

1161
00:53:34,778 --> 00:53:37,182
Wait, you forgot the Little Sizzlers.

1162
00:53:40,418 --> 00:53:42,787
Ironically, there's no tipping allowed.

1163
00:53:46,925 --> 00:53:51,395
So, uh, how is the meal so far, good?

1164
00:53:51,462 --> 00:53:54,298
Show's over, nothing to see here.

1165
00:53:55,567 --> 00:53:57,602
What is this place, and who are you?

1166
00:53:57,702 --> 00:53:59,803
[Joel] Well, this is here, and I'm me.

1167
00:53:59,870 --> 00:54:01,940
Remember, I told you last night?

1168
00:54:02,507 --> 00:54:03,708
Did you tell me?

1169
00:54:03,775 --> 00:54:06,043
Well, I was really crocked last night.

1170
00:54:06,110 --> 00:54:07,912
Yes, I'm Dr. Eric Vornoff.

1171
00:54:07,978 --> 00:54:09,247
[Joel] What did I do? What did I say?

1172
00:54:09,313 --> 00:54:12,550
-Did somebody bring my roommate home?
-This is the old Willow's place.

1173
00:54:12,617 --> 00:54:13,618
Yes.

1174
00:54:14,953 --> 00:54:16,220
[Joel] Watch me rock.

1175
00:54:16,822 --> 00:54:18,457
How did you know?

1176
00:54:18,523 --> 00:54:20,158
I checked our real estate files.

1177
00:54:20,558 --> 00:54:22,760
A Dr. Eric Vornoff
bought the Willow's place

1178
00:54:22,827 --> 00:54:24,828
in November of 1948.

1179
00:54:24,895 --> 00:54:26,965
You've got spunk.
I hate spunk.

1180
00:54:27,032 --> 00:54:30,535
-You are a newspaper reporter, huh?
-Yes.

1181
00:54:31,435 --> 00:54:34,338
My name seems to mean much to you.

1182
00:54:35,407 --> 00:54:38,410
Well, actually, it wasn't
your name I was interested in.

1183
00:54:38,477 --> 00:54:41,078
[Tom] We're doing
a tempo feature on Lobo.

1184
00:54:41,145 --> 00:54:43,515
And the Willow's place
being right on the lake,

1185
00:54:44,415 --> 00:54:46,652
I looked in the files to see
what happened to it.

1186
00:54:47,585 --> 00:54:50,055
Your name was there as the purchaser.

1187
00:54:50,122 --> 00:54:52,090
-Oh, mama.
-I thought perhaps you might have

1188
00:54:52,157 --> 00:54:56,662
-heard something about the monster.
-[laughs] My dear Ms. Lawton. I--

1189
00:54:56,728 --> 00:54:58,930
When did I tell you my name?

1190
00:54:58,997 --> 00:55:00,932
-[Tom] Todd's house.
-You didn't.

1191
00:55:02,000 --> 00:55:04,202
But since you were unconscious,

1192
00:55:05,503 --> 00:55:08,407
I took the liberty
looking into your purse.

1193
00:55:08,473 --> 00:55:10,475
You must have seen my press card.

1194
00:55:10,542 --> 00:55:11,843
Yes, I did.

1195
00:55:11,943 --> 00:55:14,412
-[Joel] And where are my Tic Tacs?
-Since you knew the answers already,

1196
00:55:14,478 --> 00:55:17,082
why the third degree,
and who or what was that monster?

1197
00:55:17,148 --> 00:55:19,717
Oh, he's quite human.

1198
00:55:19,783 --> 00:55:22,487
I discovered him
in the wilderness of Tibet.

1199
00:55:22,553 --> 00:55:24,788
He's been quite useful to me.

1200
00:55:24,855 --> 00:55:26,023
[Tom] Well, OK.

1201
00:55:29,460 --> 00:55:31,528
Ah, how do I get into this painting?

1202
00:55:34,598 --> 00:55:37,835
But now, since your tiring experience,

1203
00:55:37,902 --> 00:55:40,638
I think we have talked too much.

1204
00:55:40,705 --> 00:55:42,307
I'm not the least bit tired.

1205
00:55:42,373 --> 00:55:44,742
[Joel] I could talk to you for hours.

1206
00:55:44,842 --> 00:55:46,778
-[all] Ahh!
-Juicy.

1207
00:55:51,582 --> 00:55:53,618
[Tom] Oh, not with the hand again.

1208
00:55:53,685 --> 00:55:58,322
-You are so tired.
-[Tom] Ah-wub-wub-wub.

1209
00:55:58,390 --> 00:56:00,225
A-wub-wub-wub-wub.

1210
00:56:00,292 --> 00:56:01,727
Nyuk, nyuk.

1211
00:56:02,327 --> 00:56:04,095
Have to puke.

1212
00:56:05,663 --> 00:56:07,565
Well, that'll put you off your eggs, huh?

1213
00:56:08,198 --> 00:56:10,368
I do feel very tired.

1214
00:56:10,468 --> 00:56:13,237
[Joel] This could be upside down,
for all we know.

1215
00:56:16,107 --> 00:56:18,610
Suddenly, I feel blurry.

1216
00:56:18,677 --> 00:56:19,943
Tired.

1217
00:56:20,145 --> 00:56:22,747
[Joel] Excellent.
Can I have your kipper snack?

1218
00:56:22,813 --> 00:56:25,015
Very tired.

1219
00:56:27,018 --> 00:56:28,018
[Tom] Crotch!

1220
00:56:28,085 --> 00:56:30,255
Hey, hey, Bela,
don't even think about it.

1221
00:56:31,288 --> 00:56:34,292
[Joel] Eh, time for your whipping, boy.
Come here.

1222
00:56:36,627 --> 00:56:38,530
-Lobo!
-[Tom] Bus table two, please.

1223
00:56:38,597 --> 00:56:40,765
Take the girl to my quarters.

1224
00:56:44,702 --> 00:56:46,137
Hey, Mr. Douglas.

1225
00:56:48,138 --> 00:56:49,573
[Joel] Um...

1226
00:56:49,640 --> 00:56:52,110
Anybody need a Chevy, here?

1227
00:56:53,778 --> 00:56:55,480
Hey, the door changed.

1228
00:56:56,313 --> 00:56:59,817
[Tom] Looks like
the Cabin of Doctor Caligari. [chuckles]

1229
00:56:59,883 --> 00:57:02,787
Hello!
I'm the worthless ancillary character,

1230
00:57:02,853 --> 00:57:04,388
waiting to be killed off.

1231
00:57:04,455 --> 00:57:05,790
Hello!

1232
00:57:06,023 --> 00:57:09,260
[Joel] Hello?
Anybody here need a psycho-analyst?

1233
00:57:09,327 --> 00:57:11,428
-[Tom] Burl Ives!
-Elmore Leonard!

1234
00:57:11,495 --> 00:57:14,165
- [Joel] Lenin!
-[Tom] McCartney.

1235
00:57:16,233 --> 00:57:20,238
Hmm.
I wonder if this door leads to my doom.

1236
00:57:20,305 --> 00:57:22,173
Hello? Hello?

1237
00:57:22,540 --> 00:57:24,508
Hey, it's occupado!

1238
00:57:24,575 --> 00:57:27,778
Sorry, this is-- thing.
Hello?

1239
00:57:27,845 --> 00:57:30,348
Nice pictures on the wall.

1240
00:57:31,548 --> 00:57:32,683
Hello?

1241
00:57:32,950 --> 00:57:34,018
Hello?

1242
00:57:34,085 --> 00:57:36,120
Anything that's through these doors?

1243
00:57:38,355 --> 00:57:39,957
Hell-- hello.

1244
00:57:42,760 --> 00:57:43,928
[Joel] Oh, hi, Fred.

1245
00:57:44,262 --> 00:57:45,697
Wow, bumper pool.

1246
00:57:45,763 --> 00:57:47,232
Dolbert Hendricks, cool!

1247
00:57:47,298 --> 00:57:50,502
[Tom] Oh, there's a large, balding man
who might be of assistance.

1248
00:57:50,568 --> 00:57:53,037
Sir, do you think I could--
[makes choking sound]

1249
00:57:55,307 --> 00:57:57,075
Tonight, on Later with Bob Costas,

1250
00:57:57,142 --> 00:57:59,010
Bob welcomes Hans Conried.

1251
00:57:59,943 --> 00:58:01,412
-[Tom] Hands down.
- [Joel] Hey, look.

1252
00:58:01,478 --> 00:58:03,982
-Miss Kitty's changing back there.
-[Tom] Mmm.

1253
00:58:09,487 --> 00:58:11,588
God, I look good in Aviators.

1254
00:58:11,655 --> 00:58:14,392
[Joel] Good day, General Grant.
Hello?

1255
00:58:17,595 --> 00:58:19,463
[Tom] Oh, scar's healing nicely, Lobo.

1256
00:58:23,668 --> 00:58:25,937
Time to do my Cagney.

1257
00:58:26,003 --> 00:58:27,572
You dirty rat.

1258
00:58:27,638 --> 00:58:29,273
You kill brother.

1259
00:58:29,340 --> 00:58:30,808
Thank. I out of here.

1260
00:58:33,410 --> 00:58:36,647
[Joel] I love the antiques you can get
in these little out of the way places.

1261
00:58:36,713 --> 00:58:38,650
They're so good. Mmm.

1262
00:58:43,087 --> 00:58:44,188
Cato.

1263
00:58:44,855 --> 00:58:47,625
My dear Professor Strowski.

1264
00:58:47,692 --> 00:58:50,060
[Joel] My darling Dr. Lamb Chop.

1265
00:58:50,762 --> 00:58:54,198
It has been a very long time.

1266
00:58:54,298 --> 00:58:56,367
[Tom] ♪ Been a long
Lonely, lonely, lonely, lonely ♪

1267
00:58:56,433 --> 00:58:57,502
It is you.

1268
00:58:57,568 --> 00:58:59,670
[Joel] Put her in the vise.

1269
00:59:00,305 --> 00:59:02,407
In Paris, I missed you buy a month.

1270
00:59:02,473 --> 00:59:03,675
In London, a week.

1271
00:59:03,742 --> 00:59:05,410
At Loch Ness, by only one day.

1272
00:59:05,477 --> 00:59:06,743
Indianapolis, by a second.

1273
00:59:06,810 --> 00:59:09,580
The monster of Lake Marsh
sounded again like you.

1274
00:59:09,647 --> 00:59:13,685
Why the sudden interest in me,
Professor Strowski?

1275
00:59:15,453 --> 00:59:16,922
[Joel belches]

1276
00:59:16,988 --> 00:59:19,990
Your experiments in atomic energy,
of course.

1277
00:59:20,392 --> 00:59:22,427
[Joel] Why, he's an associate justice.

1278
00:59:22,493 --> 00:59:26,363
My dear country now believes in my work,

1279
00:59:26,563 --> 00:59:28,833
and that it can be a success.

1280
00:59:28,933 --> 00:59:31,502
Our government wants you to return,
Vornoff.

1281
00:59:31,602 --> 00:59:33,503
To continue your research
and experiments there.

1282
00:59:33,570 --> 00:59:35,038
Accent! Accent!

1283
00:59:35,105 --> 00:59:37,042
You can have everything at your disposal.

1284
00:59:37,108 --> 00:59:39,077
-[Tom] Everything!
-[laughs]

1285
00:59:39,510 --> 00:59:40,845
Why do you laugh?

1286
00:59:40,912 --> 00:59:42,547
Surely, this is not a laughing matter.

1287
00:59:42,613 --> 00:59:45,717
-[Joel] Well, there are funny elements.
-My dear Professor Strowski,

1288
00:59:47,185 --> 00:59:48,753
twenty years ago--

1289
00:59:48,820 --> 00:59:51,855
-Today, Sergeant Pepper Pot.
-I was banned from my homeland.

1290
00:59:52,657 --> 00:59:54,792
Parted from my wife and son,

1291
00:59:54,858 --> 00:59:57,628
- [Joel] And banned from MTV.
-Never to see them again.

1292
00:59:58,162 --> 01:00:00,097
-Why?
-Because we like you.

1293
01:00:00,163 --> 01:00:03,567
Because I suggested
to use the atom elements

1294
01:00:03,635 --> 01:00:06,137
for producing super beings.

1295
01:00:06,203 --> 01:00:11,142
-[Tom] Look at those bags.
-Beings of unthinkable strength and size.

1296
01:00:11,208 --> 01:00:12,377
[Joel] Wax beans.

1297
01:00:12,443 --> 01:00:13,443
Fava beans.

1298
01:00:13,510 --> 01:00:15,380
I was classed as a madman.

1299
01:00:15,447 --> 01:00:16,747
And I love it.

1300
01:00:16,813 --> 01:00:18,048
A charlatan.

1301
01:00:18,115 --> 01:00:20,150
[Tom] And the ladies like it, too.

1302
01:00:20,217 --> 01:00:24,255
Outlawed in a world of science,
which previously honored me as a genius.

1303
01:00:24,322 --> 01:00:25,990
I'm a genius! Thank you!

1304
01:00:26,057 --> 01:00:31,762
Now, here, in this forsaken jungle hell,

1305
01:00:31,828 --> 01:00:34,698
I have proven that I am all right.

1306
01:00:35,633 --> 01:00:37,602
[Joel] Well I'm going to take off.

1307
01:00:37,668 --> 01:00:39,870
Now, Professor Strowski,

1308
01:00:39,937 --> 01:00:43,540
-it is no laughing matter.
-[Tom pretends to snore]

1309
01:00:43,607 --> 01:00:44,775
Yes.

1310
01:00:46,010 --> 01:00:49,213
-Yes, indeed, it was a tragic error.
- [Joel] But it was fun.

1311
01:00:49,280 --> 01:00:52,417
But as soon as I learned
how correct your findings were,

1312
01:00:52,483 --> 01:00:53,985
always have been.

1313
01:00:54,052 --> 01:00:56,853
-I informed those in authority.
-[Tom] And I took the credit.

1314
01:00:56,920 --> 01:01:00,223
Vornoff, I have searched
for you everywhere,

1315
01:01:00,290 --> 01:01:02,960
everywhere I hear stories of monsters.

1316
01:01:03,027 --> 01:01:05,228
[Tom] Except here.
It's always the last place I look.

1317
01:01:05,295 --> 01:01:08,565
Now I'm here, sent to bring you home.

1318
01:01:09,300 --> 01:01:11,602
- [Joel] Oh, poopy.
-Home?

1319
01:01:13,670 --> 01:01:17,808
[Tom] Ooh, and then Brian died,
and he was such a good football player.

1320
01:01:17,875 --> 01:01:20,210
And Gale Sayers was all alone
in the world.

1321
01:01:20,277 --> 01:01:22,847
And they had the same
front room as Bewitched.

1322
01:01:23,047 --> 01:01:24,915
[Crow shuddering]

1323
01:01:25,348 --> 01:01:26,950
I have no home.

1324
01:01:27,017 --> 01:01:29,220
[Tom] I'm a lonely boy.

1325
01:01:29,587 --> 01:01:31,588
-Hunted.
- [Joel] Oh, yes.

1326
01:01:31,955 --> 01:01:34,592
-Despised.
-[Joel] That's right, bring it home, man.

1327
01:01:34,658 --> 01:01:38,428
-Mm-hmm.
-Living like an animal.

1328
01:01:38,495 --> 01:01:41,198
Well, you won't have Bela
to kick around anymore.

1329
01:01:42,233 --> 01:01:44,702
[Joel] Man, he's really
chewing the scenery.

1330
01:01:44,735 --> 01:01:48,505
I will show the world
that I can be its master!

1331
01:01:48,572 --> 01:01:50,475
[Tom]
Beastmaster: Through the Portal of Time?

1332
01:01:50,775 --> 01:01:54,712
I will perfect my own race of people.

1333
01:01:54,778 --> 01:01:56,880
Rice of people?
The San Francisco treat?

1334
01:01:56,947 --> 01:01:58,015
[Tom] Ding-ding.

1335
01:01:58,082 --> 01:02:02,320
Atomic supermen
which will conquer the world.

1336
01:02:03,955 --> 01:02:05,723
[Joel] So, anyway, that's my dream.
And you?

1337
01:02:11,862 --> 01:02:13,097
Yes.

1338
01:02:13,765 --> 01:02:15,633
Yes, of course.

1339
01:02:15,700 --> 01:02:18,870
A truly great master race.

1340
01:02:18,937 --> 01:02:23,273
Such as I convinced my superiors
only you could create.

1341
01:02:23,340 --> 01:02:27,945
One with which our government can
rule the world without debate.

1342
01:02:28,045 --> 01:02:32,550
-[Tom] Are you nuts?
-You misunderstand me, Strowski.

1343
01:02:33,985 --> 01:02:37,020
I don't intend to return home.

1344
01:02:37,087 --> 01:02:38,790
Not till Mom says she's sorry.

1345
01:02:38,857 --> 01:02:43,093
My plans are for myself.

1346
01:02:43,193 --> 01:02:44,995
-[Tom] OK.
-Are you mad, Vornoff?

1347
01:02:45,062 --> 01:02:47,097
[Joel] What the hell do you think?

1348
01:02:47,165 --> 01:02:49,667
One is always considered mad

1349
01:02:50,400 --> 01:02:54,238
when one discovered something
that others cannot grasp.

1350
01:02:54,305 --> 01:02:55,338
[Tom] Like tofu.

1351
01:02:57,275 --> 01:02:59,177
[Joel] Look out, I've got a gun!

1352
01:02:59,243 --> 01:03:00,745
Oh.

1353
01:03:00,812 --> 01:03:03,013
Our government ordered me
to bring you back.

1354
01:03:03,080 --> 01:03:04,082
[Tom] Ooh.

1355
01:03:05,048 --> 01:03:09,220
I'm afraid you'll find that
rather difficult.

1356
01:03:09,287 --> 01:03:11,222
Time to go to happy hour.

1357
01:03:11,288 --> 01:03:13,723
I did not come alone.

1358
01:03:13,790 --> 01:03:16,860
Neither did I, my dear Strowski.

1359
01:03:16,927 --> 01:03:17,995
Bring him along, Lobo.

1360
01:03:18,062 --> 01:03:21,065
[Joel] How does Lobo sneak up on anyone?

1361
01:03:21,798 --> 01:03:23,600
[Tom] It's Lobo the Scalia.

1362
01:03:25,068 --> 01:03:26,170
Come, come.

1363
01:03:26,770 --> 01:03:28,840
You know, you take a chance
with any bed and breakfast.

1364
01:03:28,940 --> 01:03:30,908
[Joel] Yeah, Lobo's wearing Zubaz.

1365
01:03:31,975 --> 01:03:33,845
[Tom] Ooh, I've got to follow that?

1366
01:03:33,912 --> 01:03:35,613
Bleh! Literally.

1367
01:03:35,680 --> 01:03:38,883
-You-- you may kill me...
- [Joel] Hugs.

1368
01:03:39,383 --> 01:03:41,818
...but you will never get away with it.

1369
01:03:41,885 --> 01:03:44,855
Vornoff, no! Vornoff, no!

1370
01:03:45,890 --> 01:03:47,925
[Joel] Not the beanbag chair!

1371
01:03:51,895 --> 01:03:54,998
Ha! We do have fun.
Sorry about the whipping earlier.

1372
01:03:56,433 --> 01:03:59,737
Vornoff! Vornoff! Vornoff!

1373
01:03:59,803 --> 01:04:02,640
[Tom] Yes, it's good this time,
isn't it, friend?

1374
01:04:04,208 --> 01:04:08,578
-Now, Professor Strowski...
-[Crow] And Hutch.

1375
01:04:08,645 --> 01:04:12,583
...you will get a much closer view
of the product of my genius.

1376
01:04:12,683 --> 01:04:14,385
[Joel] Come on, turn around, look at it.

1377
01:04:14,452 --> 01:04:16,120
It's your death, come on.

1378
01:04:16,887 --> 01:04:20,692
The alleged monster of Lake Marsh.

1379
01:04:21,592 --> 01:04:24,695
[Tom] Well, it's allegedly killing him.

1380
01:04:25,930 --> 01:04:28,198
[laughing] How do we get away with this?

1381
01:04:28,865 --> 01:04:32,470
Your country offers fame and fortune.

1382
01:04:32,537 --> 01:04:36,040
[all] ♪ Fame and fortune
Fame and fortune ♪

1383
01:04:36,073 --> 01:04:39,910
Life is so much more great.

1384
01:04:39,977 --> 01:04:42,513
[Joel] Hey! Quit playing with your food.

1385
01:04:42,580 --> 01:04:44,215
German food is so heavy.

1386
01:04:44,282 --> 01:04:46,883
You will disappear, Professor Strowski,

1387
01:04:46,950 --> 01:04:50,153
just as all the others have disappeared.

1388
01:04:50,220 --> 01:04:51,888
[Tom] Hey, Lobo's got his old scar back.

1389
01:04:53,657 --> 01:04:56,060
- [Joel] Shall we?
-After you.

1390
01:04:56,127 --> 01:04:58,930
[Tom] It's fun for five minutes,
but then it gets old.

1391
01:05:00,097 --> 01:05:02,367
[Joel] There, surround sound.

1392
01:05:06,070 --> 01:05:08,973
[Tom] Yep, stick a fork in him.
Turn him over. He's done.

1393
01:05:12,577 --> 01:05:15,680
[Joel] Man, I wish they had a more
effective monster. It's taking forever.

1394
01:05:15,747 --> 01:05:16,947
Done and done.

1395
01:05:20,517 --> 01:05:22,320
[Tom] Uh, Marty?

1396
01:05:22,387 --> 01:05:25,155
Marty, let's just park on the ramp, OK?
Please?

1397
01:05:30,928 --> 01:05:32,963
[Joel] Hey, wow, I found another car.

1398
01:05:33,030 --> 01:05:34,498
Second one today!

1399
01:05:34,565 --> 01:05:35,833
[Tom] Studebaker.

1400
01:05:39,570 --> 01:05:41,805
I thought we could just talk for a while.

1401
01:05:44,575 --> 01:05:46,810
[Tom] Oh, my God, it's a car.

1402
01:05:46,877 --> 01:05:49,413
-Think it's the one Strowski used?
-I'd make book it is.

1403
01:05:49,480 --> 01:05:51,615
- [Joel] Huh?
-It's getting dark now.

1404
01:05:52,182 --> 01:05:54,185
You take the car and go down to the beach.

1405
01:05:54,252 --> 01:05:56,453
I'll go through the swamp
to the old Willow's place.

1406
01:05:56,520 --> 01:05:57,522
Ok.

1407
01:05:57,588 --> 01:06:00,423
-Oh, here. Better take this with you.
-Right.

1408
01:06:00,490 --> 01:06:02,192
-Oh, Marty.
-What do you want to do tonight?

1409
01:06:02,258 --> 01:06:04,295
If you see anything suspicious,
you know where I am.

1410
01:06:04,562 --> 01:06:06,530
Trouble is, everything in this swamp
is suspicious.

1411
01:06:06,597 --> 01:06:09,100
[Joel laughs] Get out of here, you nut.

1412
01:06:11,768 --> 01:06:13,437
[Tom] Buh-bye, now.

1413
01:06:14,272 --> 01:06:15,272
Damn, I like him.

1414
01:06:15,338 --> 01:06:16,340
He's a good egg.

1415
01:06:16,373 --> 01:06:18,042
Wish he felt he could open up to me.

1416
01:06:20,845 --> 01:06:22,680
[Joel] Well... back at it.

1417
01:06:27,452 --> 01:06:29,420
Hey, I hear a bassoon back here.

1418
01:06:30,587 --> 01:06:32,322
[Tom] Oh, looks like a clue--
Oh, no.

1419
01:06:32,390 --> 01:06:33,690
Maybe that could be a-- No.

1420
01:06:33,757 --> 01:06:35,893
That's not a-- Maybe-- No.

1421
01:06:37,227 --> 01:06:40,463
-Short night's journey into day.
-[Tom] Uh-huh.

1422
01:06:44,135 --> 01:06:47,203
[Joel] Whoa!
He fell into the Billy Barty trap.

1423
01:06:49,040 --> 01:06:52,175
[Tom] Hey, I pressed the "up" button.
Really.

1424
01:06:55,412 --> 01:06:56,580
[Joel] I don't believe this.

1425
01:06:56,647 --> 01:06:58,282
It actually worked.

1426
01:07:05,723 --> 01:07:08,392
[Tom] I remember when rock was young.
Ha-ha.

1427
01:07:08,858 --> 01:07:10,862
He's shooting at a different movie.

1428
01:07:18,668 --> 01:07:20,805
[Joel] Oh, sheesh!

1429
01:07:20,872 --> 01:07:22,540
Brother.

1430
01:07:26,677 --> 01:07:28,578
How many shots does he have?

1431
01:07:28,778 --> 01:07:30,815
[Tom] Yeah, here.
Well, this thing isn't helping me much.

1432
01:07:32,583 --> 01:07:34,918
[Joel] Well, you know,
I was going to help you.

1433
01:07:42,192 --> 01:07:44,528
Phew! That was really quicksand.

1434
01:07:44,595 --> 01:07:46,130
Man, was that quicksand.

1435
01:07:46,197 --> 01:07:47,965
-[Tom] Real quick.
-That's sand.

1436
01:07:48,032 --> 01:07:50,500
[Tom] So what do you suppose a crocodile
was doing in Florida?

1437
01:07:50,567 --> 01:07:51,735
[Joel] Retiring.

1438
01:07:55,205 --> 01:07:57,975
Well, go, for crying out loud! Geez.

1439
01:07:58,042 --> 01:07:59,810
-[Tom] Geez.
-[Joel] Gall.

1440
01:08:00,343 --> 01:08:03,047
[Tom] ♪ It's the new zoo review ♪

1441
01:08:03,113 --> 01:08:05,315
[humming]

1442
01:08:05,382 --> 01:08:07,718
-I'm in here.
-[Tom] Sorry.

1443
01:08:10,187 --> 01:08:12,623
-[Tillie] What can I do for you, sir?
-Captain Robbins, homicide.

1444
01:08:12,690 --> 01:08:13,890
-[Tom] Can you get it?
-[Joel] Get it.

1445
01:08:13,957 --> 01:08:17,193
Why, Captain, I haven't murdered anyone
in a month of Sundays.

1446
01:08:17,293 --> 01:08:19,963
Did Janet Lawton stop by
here late yesterday afternoon?

1447
01:08:20,030 --> 01:08:22,633
Oh, just like a policeman,
no sense of humor.

1448
01:08:22,700 --> 01:08:24,100
Yes, she stopped by late in the afternoon.

1449
01:08:24,168 --> 01:08:25,768
-Hey, her pencil's gone.
-Remember the time?

1450
01:08:25,837 --> 01:08:27,103
-[woman] Not to the second.
-It's back.

1451
01:08:27,170 --> 01:08:30,273
She came in around 2:00
and left around 3:30.

1452
01:08:30,340 --> 01:08:31,808
And again, that isn't so late, is it?

1453
01:08:31,875 --> 01:08:34,312
-What'd she want?
-She was looking for information.

1454
01:08:34,378 --> 01:08:36,980
That I'm sure of. Do you happen to know
what that information was?

1455
01:08:37,047 --> 01:08:38,348
Well, sure.

1456
01:08:38,415 --> 01:08:39,450
Well?

1457
01:08:39,517 --> 01:08:41,085
-Well, what?
-Must we play games?

1458
01:08:41,152 --> 01:08:42,318
I didn't know we were.

1459
01:08:42,385 --> 01:08:44,355
What was Miss Lawton
looking for in the files?

1460
01:08:44,422 --> 01:08:46,523
Oh. Well, why didn't you ask that
in the first place,

1461
01:08:46,590 --> 01:08:48,792
-instead of all your chit-chat?
-[Tom] Doh.

1462
01:08:48,858 --> 01:08:51,128
-She was looking over...
-It's gone again. The pencil.

1463
01:08:51,195 --> 01:08:54,865
...the real estate files in October,
November, and December of 1948.

1464
01:08:54,932 --> 01:08:58,302
In particular, the sale of the old
Willow's place out on Lake Marsh.

1465
01:08:58,368 --> 01:08:59,637
You can tell they're in love.

1466
01:08:59,703 --> 01:09:00,972
Did she find what she was looking for?

1467
01:09:01,038 --> 01:09:02,272
-Well, I guess she did.
-Oh.

1468
01:09:02,338 --> 01:09:04,073
-At least she found the sale notice.
-Gone again!

1469
01:09:04,140 --> 01:09:06,343
I saw it myself after she rushed out.

1470
01:09:06,410 --> 01:09:09,447
She left the paper open at the November
26th date. Want to see it?

1471
01:09:09,513 --> 01:09:10,747
-Why, very much.
-It's back.

1472
01:09:10,813 --> 01:09:13,117
Well, it's right up there.
I haven't had time to put it back, yet.

1473
01:09:13,183 --> 01:09:17,020
[Joel] What's this, here? It says "weird
fingerless bird man under suspicion"?

1474
01:09:17,087 --> 01:09:18,688
Well-- What-- Oh?

1475
01:09:19,690 --> 01:09:22,358
-Yeah, Dr. Eric Vornoff.
-[Tom] Mm-hmm.

1476
01:09:22,660 --> 01:09:23,827
Give me all you have on him.

1477
01:09:23,893 --> 01:09:25,462
-[Tom] Ah, he's called his budgie.
-[Joel] Hmm.

1478
01:09:27,063 --> 01:09:28,165
I'll hang on.

1479
01:09:31,268 --> 01:09:33,037
Uh, my turn, boss.

1480
01:09:36,340 --> 01:09:38,142
[Joel] Hey, go get Daddy a beer, eh?

1481
01:09:38,208 --> 01:09:39,443
Are we ready for the girl?

1482
01:09:39,610 --> 01:09:41,212
[Tom] Go set the table, honey, will ya?

1483
01:09:44,615 --> 01:09:47,083
Oy, this arthritis is killing me.

1484
01:09:48,418 --> 01:09:51,022
[Joel] And this right here
is my fork ball.

1485
01:09:52,322 --> 01:09:54,358
[Tom] Yeah, this is my slider.

1486
01:09:54,425 --> 01:09:56,193
Really falls off the table,
let me tell ya.

1487
01:10:01,298 --> 01:10:02,700
[Joel] I must. I must.

1488
01:10:02,767 --> 01:10:04,033
I must increase my bust.

1489
01:10:04,100 --> 01:10:06,237
The bigger the better, the tighter
my sweater, the boys depend on us.

1490
01:10:06,303 --> 01:10:08,238
Hey. I must. I must--

1491
01:10:08,305 --> 01:10:10,975
Ladies and gentlemen,
the June Taylor dance--

1492
01:10:11,108 --> 01:10:12,643
Oh, geez.

1493
01:10:12,710 --> 01:10:14,545
[Tom] Ew. Yech.

1494
01:10:15,178 --> 01:10:17,547
[Tom] ♪ He is now to be among you ♪

1495
01:10:28,492 --> 01:10:29,727
Boy, do I have a headache.

1496
01:10:29,793 --> 01:10:31,962
I got a head crammed full of bad ideas.

1497
01:10:32,028 --> 01:10:33,363
I shouldn't have watched so much.

1498
01:10:33,430 --> 01:10:34,765
You know, I'm beginning to think

1499
01:10:34,832 --> 01:10:37,433
the world would be a better place
with no advertising.

1500
01:10:38,535 --> 01:10:39,937
No advertising?

1501
01:10:40,003 --> 01:10:41,205
Uh, who are you?

1502
01:10:41,272 --> 01:10:45,408
Why, I'm Willie, the wonderful,
whimsical, wise-cracking waffle.

1503
01:10:45,475 --> 01:10:47,477
-Waffle?
-Hi, Waffle.

1504
01:10:47,543 --> 01:10:50,280
I was just wishing our world
was without advertising.

1505
01:10:50,880 --> 01:10:53,317
Say, didn't you appear
in the same getup in show 317,

1506
01:10:53,383 --> 01:10:55,052
Viking Women vs. The Sea Serpent?

1507
01:10:55,118 --> 01:10:57,287
-It was all I had, I had to work fast.
-Oh.

1508
01:10:57,353 --> 01:11:01,225
Say, I wonder what kind of paint
Pat Summerall buys.

1509
01:11:01,292 --> 01:11:03,360
No advertising. [whistle]

1510
01:11:03,427 --> 01:11:06,030
Wow, I had no idea,
Magical Waffle Womper.

1511
01:11:06,097 --> 01:11:07,932
Knew your father, I did.

1512
01:11:07,998 --> 01:11:10,667
Golly, I sure got a hankering
to watch something

1513
01:11:10,733 --> 01:11:14,270
from the mind of Billy Crystal,
but I have no idea where to turn.

1514
01:11:14,338 --> 01:11:16,273
-No advertising.
-Wow.

1515
01:11:16,340 --> 01:11:17,975
The hell--

1516
01:11:18,042 --> 01:11:20,310
Ah, oh, Magical Waffle Cobbler,

1517
01:11:20,377 --> 01:11:22,512
I sure wish that snotty British guy

1518
01:11:22,578 --> 01:11:25,848
would write some more poetry
about GAP jeans.

1519
01:11:25,915 --> 01:11:27,985
-No advertising.
-Yes!

1520
01:11:28,785 --> 01:11:31,888
Hey, Mr. Wafflecrank,
I wonder where John Madden shops.

1521
01:11:31,955 --> 01:11:33,790
No advertising. [whistle]

1522
01:11:33,857 --> 01:11:36,527
Wow, you know, it's as if
Ace Hardware commercials never existed.

1523
01:11:36,593 --> 01:11:37,593
Right, Tommy.

1524
01:11:37,662 --> 01:11:40,730
Oh, Waffle Weenie,
if you took away advertising,

1525
01:11:40,797 --> 01:11:43,433
my sneakers would cost a fraction
of what they cost now.

1526
01:11:43,500 --> 01:11:45,002
-No advertising.
-Yes!

1527
01:11:45,068 --> 01:11:46,937
-[whistle]
-Hey, Mr. Waffle Moppet,

1528
01:11:47,003 --> 01:11:49,940
I want the rain forest
to last for another two billion years.

1529
01:11:49,973 --> 01:11:51,942
Oh, all right.
No advertising. [whistle]

1530
01:11:52,008 --> 01:11:54,812
Oh, boy, I never realized.
It must be so cool.

1531
01:11:54,878 --> 01:11:57,180
[sighs] Advertising.

1532
01:11:57,247 --> 01:11:59,617
Enjoy some now. [whistle]

1533
01:12:06,057 --> 01:12:07,290
[screaming]

1534
01:12:09,827 --> 01:12:12,830
[Joel] You're spoiling my wedding day,
boss. Think of the guests.

1535
01:12:12,897 --> 01:12:14,732
I command you.

1536
01:12:16,767 --> 01:12:19,803
[Joel] Oh, man.
Somebody took the TV listings.

1537
01:12:19,870 --> 01:12:21,205
[Tom] Yeah, wha-- Huh?

1538
01:12:24,908 --> 01:12:25,908
[Tom] Nice case.

1539
01:12:30,413 --> 01:12:35,218
[Tom] This man is the same as this man,
only in a different pose.

1540
01:12:35,285 --> 01:12:36,453
Your mission, Jim--

1541
01:12:42,692 --> 01:12:43,693
Huh?

1542
01:12:44,762 --> 01:12:45,995
[Tom] Oh.

1543
01:12:46,063 --> 01:12:48,798
Hey, cool, you can flush the fireplace.

1544
01:12:49,567 --> 01:12:52,770
[Joel] You know,
I bet Santa appreciates this.

1545
01:12:56,573 --> 01:12:57,573
[Tom] Ooh, ow.

1546
01:13:00,977 --> 01:13:02,880
This is a weird spa.

1547
01:13:03,713 --> 01:13:06,217
[Joel] Hi, Uncle Bela. Aunt Tor.

1548
01:13:08,352 --> 01:13:10,853
Get the equipment ready.

1549
01:13:10,920 --> 01:13:13,457
[Tom] If anyone one can find
just cause to stop this movie,

1550
01:13:13,523 --> 01:13:15,425
speak now or forever hold your peace.

1551
01:13:15,492 --> 01:13:16,927
[Joel] Uh-- Oh.

1552
01:13:22,432 --> 01:13:24,768
And bite down, please.

1553
01:13:24,835 --> 01:13:26,437
Just for a moment.

1554
01:13:27,203 --> 01:13:30,340
And then, you will emerge a woman

1555
01:13:30,407 --> 01:13:33,010
of super strength and beauty.

1556
01:13:33,077 --> 01:13:35,578
[Joel] You will inspire Helen Reddy.

1557
01:13:36,880 --> 01:13:38,082
You're insane.

1558
01:13:38,148 --> 01:13:39,750
-[Tom] Yeah.
-My paper knows where I am.

1559
01:13:39,817 --> 01:13:41,818
You can't hurt me
and get away with it.

1560
01:13:41,885 --> 01:13:44,888
As soon as my experiments are completed,

1561
01:13:44,955 --> 01:13:46,923
no one can ever touch me.

1562
01:13:46,990 --> 01:13:49,627
I will make the law.

1563
01:13:50,427 --> 01:13:53,597
Either way, whether I succeed or fail...

1564
01:13:53,663 --> 01:13:56,667
-[Tom] Stay tuned.
-...it will not hurt you very long.

1565
01:13:56,733 --> 01:13:57,868
[Janet] Let me go!

1566
01:13:57,935 --> 01:13:59,368
Let me go!

1567
01:13:59,435 --> 01:14:01,205
[Dick] You heard what she said.

1568
01:14:01,272 --> 01:14:02,605
Johnny Depp!

1569
01:14:03,440 --> 01:14:05,508
[Tom] Uh, sir,
we haven't begun seating, yet.

1570
01:14:05,608 --> 01:14:07,110
For one thing, the police.

1571
01:14:07,177 --> 01:14:08,812
Now, turn her loose, and make it fast.

1572
01:14:10,113 --> 01:14:12,115
You have the advantage for the moment.

1573
01:14:12,182 --> 01:14:14,383
[Joel] Until my stealthy giant appears.

1574
01:14:22,592 --> 01:14:24,728
-Dick!
-No, duck!

1575
01:14:24,795 --> 01:14:26,263
Damn, I said that wrong.

1576
01:14:30,833 --> 01:14:31,935
[Tom] Geez, get this guy.

1577
01:14:32,002 --> 01:14:33,903
Tor, could you help me move next week?

1578
01:14:35,472 --> 01:14:37,807
[Joel] Hooker,
you've gone too far this time.

1579
01:14:37,875 --> 01:14:39,075
[Tom] Hooker's a good cop.

1580
01:14:44,582 --> 01:14:47,450
-[Tom humming to soundtrack]
-[Crow] OK, everybody remember that tree.

1581
01:14:49,118 --> 01:14:50,487
[Tom] What tree?

1582
01:14:50,553 --> 01:14:53,123
[Joel] Well, I'm glad we all
rode together. That was fun.

1583
01:14:53,723 --> 01:14:56,660
[Tom] You know, the generic police
are just as good as the brand name ones,

1584
01:14:56,727 --> 01:14:58,628
but they're cheaper.

1585
01:14:58,695 --> 01:14:59,763
Where's Lieutenant Craig?

1586
01:14:59,830 --> 01:15:03,100
We found Strowski's car down the road.
Lieutenant took off through the swamp.

1587
01:15:03,167 --> 01:15:04,968
Sent me down here to the beach
to wait for him.

1588
01:15:05,035 --> 01:15:07,103
-Where was he heading?
-Old Willow's place.

1589
01:15:07,170 --> 01:15:08,638
-Kelton.
-Yes, sir?

1590
01:15:08,705 --> 01:15:10,040
Now, this is your first time out.

1591
01:15:10,107 --> 01:15:11,442
Don't mess up the operation.

1592
01:15:11,508 --> 01:15:14,678
-Yes, sir. I mean, no, sir.
-[Joel and bots chuckle]

1593
01:15:14,712 --> 01:15:15,845
-Martin.
-What up?

1594
01:15:15,912 --> 01:15:18,182
-Yes, sir.
-Come on.

1595
01:15:22,852 --> 01:15:25,022
[Joel] You know, these are
really quite comfortable.

1596
01:15:26,257 --> 01:15:29,893
I'm sure my experiments on the young lady
will interest you.

1597
01:15:29,960 --> 01:15:31,595
I'll live to see you hang.

1598
01:15:31,662 --> 01:15:34,197
Oh, hang to live, don't live to hang.

1599
01:15:34,263 --> 01:15:37,000
There are much more
important things to attend.

1600
01:15:37,200 --> 01:15:40,003
To-- We've got the wedding,
and reception, then--

1601
01:15:41,838 --> 01:15:43,940
[Joel] Tor, don't play with the guests.

1602
01:15:47,577 --> 01:15:50,313
[Tom] Oh, Bela,
your bedside manner is bad,

1603
01:15:50,380 --> 01:15:51,882
and your breath is worse.

1604
01:15:51,948 --> 01:15:54,885
I hope the straps are not too cutting.

1605
01:15:55,952 --> 01:15:59,088
Such lovely skin should not be marred.

1606
01:16:06,263 --> 01:16:08,965
[Joel] Let's get goofy, Tor, huh?
Come on.

1607
01:16:10,667 --> 01:16:12,035
[Tom] What do you need, boss?

1608
01:16:21,110 --> 01:16:24,047
Oh, yuck!
It's been sitting down his pants!

1609
01:16:25,615 --> 01:16:27,850
[Joel] Hey, only I get to pet that hat!

1610
01:16:33,490 --> 01:16:34,625
[screaming]

1611
01:16:34,725 --> 01:16:37,727
Tor prefers a more traditional wedding.

1612
01:16:37,793 --> 01:16:39,462
Stop!

1613
01:16:39,528 --> 01:16:40,863
Stop, I command!

1614
01:16:43,300 --> 01:16:45,602
[Tom] Tor, your review is next week,
you know.

1615
01:16:45,668 --> 01:16:47,237
This won't look on your record.

1616
01:16:47,303 --> 01:16:50,073
Tor, more step and it's a written warning.

1617
01:16:53,843 --> 01:16:56,178
Ooh, that weird lab guy flipped out.

1618
01:16:58,882 --> 01:17:03,187
[Tom] ♪ Bess, you is my woman, now ♪

1619
01:17:03,987 --> 01:17:06,022
Lobo feel free.

1620
01:17:06,923 --> 01:17:10,260
[Joel] Uh, sorry about commotion.

1621
01:17:11,962 --> 01:17:14,865
[Tom]
♪ I hear you're getting married ♪

1622
01:17:16,667 --> 01:17:19,635
[Crow] Well, we'll have you
out of this in just a second.

1623
01:17:20,870 --> 01:17:22,005
There you go.

1624
01:17:22,072 --> 01:17:23,073
Easy, Janet.

1625
01:17:23,173 --> 01:17:24,942
[Joel] Name's Tor.

1626
01:17:28,812 --> 01:17:29,813
That one.

1627
01:17:31,147 --> 01:17:33,650
[Tom pretends to cry]

1628
01:17:35,152 --> 01:17:37,820
And that--
All right, hop down, you're done.

1629
01:17:37,887 --> 01:17:41,157
Grab a lollipop and schedule your next
appointment with the receptionist.

1630
01:17:42,425 --> 01:17:44,660
[Tom] Uh, gee, Tor,
you're nice and everything.

1631
01:17:44,728 --> 01:17:45,928
I hope you understand.

1632
01:17:46,328 --> 01:17:49,532
I just kind of love you like the fat,
bald brother I never had.

1633
01:17:50,367 --> 01:17:52,502
[Joel] Uh, Lobo call Jan?

1634
01:17:52,568 --> 01:17:54,003
[Tom] Well, you know--

1635
01:17:54,537 --> 01:17:56,840
T'was Beauty confused the beast.

1636
01:17:57,473 --> 01:17:59,575
[Dick] Jan. Get me the gun.

1637
01:17:59,642 --> 01:18:01,010
[Tom] I'll get the photographer.

1638
01:18:03,647 --> 01:18:04,680
Easy.

1639
01:18:07,217 --> 01:18:08,218
Hey, Lob.

1640
01:18:11,287 --> 01:18:13,923
[Joel] Oh, that does a lot of good.
Thanks. Great.

1641
01:18:14,825 --> 01:18:17,027
[Tom] Tighter. C'mon, tighter. Yeah.

1642
01:18:20,563 --> 01:18:23,200
Uh, boss, you still owe me a paycheck.

1643
01:18:25,835 --> 01:18:28,838
[Joel] Yep, I tried.
Just throw him out back with the rest.

1644
01:18:28,905 --> 01:18:29,940
It's good, just--

1645
01:18:30,440 --> 01:18:31,975
[Tom] A big pile, now.

1646
01:18:32,775 --> 01:18:34,577
No rush, honey, we're just going to die.

1647
01:18:35,512 --> 01:18:36,980
Can I help you over there?

1648
01:18:39,750 --> 01:18:42,752
[Joel] So, how long have you been
seeing this Lobo guy?

1649
01:18:47,323 --> 01:18:49,725
[Tom] No, I see you as a blonde.

1650
01:18:51,762 --> 01:18:55,365
[Joel] Uh, batter one.
Batter two.

1651
01:18:57,900 --> 01:19:00,503
[Tom] ♪ Tor between two lovers ♪

1652
01:19:00,570 --> 01:19:02,305
This is why Tor never got raises.

1653
01:19:02,405 --> 01:19:03,607
He's so slow.

1654
01:19:03,707 --> 01:19:06,577
[Tom] Well, his attitude is,
you know, you want it done now,

1655
01:19:06,643 --> 01:19:07,777
or do you want it done right?

1656
01:19:07,977 --> 01:19:09,078
That kind of thing.

1657
01:19:09,145 --> 01:19:12,648
[Joel] Uh...
How do you work this thing, boss?

1658
01:19:14,117 --> 01:19:16,987
[Tom] Lobo take classes at DeVry.

1659
01:19:24,493 --> 01:19:27,463
-[Tom] Hey.
- [Joel] Oh, that's a Van Heusen!

1660
01:19:27,763 --> 01:19:30,000
[Tom] Look, he gave him the shirt
right off his back.

1661
01:19:33,637 --> 01:19:37,140
[Joel] Tell your friends Tor back in town.

1662
01:19:37,207 --> 01:19:39,542
Tough day. Tor hate Monday.

1663
01:19:49,118 --> 01:19:51,688
[Joel] C'mon, honey,
get back in that ring.

1664
01:19:51,755 --> 01:19:53,223
Come on.

1665
01:19:54,023 --> 01:19:56,392
[Tom] Oh, for crying--
Come on, dummy.

1666
01:19:57,627 --> 01:20:00,563
Here's a little number I whipped up
when I was in the Caribbean.

1667
01:20:00,630 --> 01:20:02,765
[Tom hums to soundtrack]

1668
01:20:02,832 --> 01:20:08,838
[all hum Klingon battle theme]

1669
01:20:10,940 --> 01:20:13,477
[Joel] Oh, for crying out-- Tor!

1670
01:20:18,582 --> 01:20:21,183
[Tom] What the-- Hey, you're bucking
for my job, aren't you?

1671
01:20:23,953 --> 01:20:25,388
No!

1672
01:20:25,455 --> 01:20:28,258
-No!
-That's the waffle iron, you idiot!

1673
01:20:33,697 --> 01:20:34,697
[Tom] Ready?

1674
01:20:36,600 --> 01:20:37,667
[Tom] Huh.

1675
01:20:38,535 --> 01:20:42,238
[Joel] Mm-hmm, that's good rain.
Hmm, hmm, hmm.

1676
01:20:43,607 --> 01:20:44,640
[Tom] Hmm.

1677
01:20:47,877 --> 01:20:50,447
I think he's learned his lesson, Lobo.

1678
01:20:51,715 --> 01:20:54,083
-What's going on?
- [Joel] I don't know.

1679
01:20:54,150 --> 01:20:57,420
But I think if I were a kid,
my mom wouldn't let me watch this.

1680
01:20:57,920 --> 01:20:59,990
[Tom] I love you, damn it. [crying]

1681
01:21:01,658 --> 01:21:04,393
You never took me to ballgames.

1682
01:21:05,695 --> 01:21:07,697
[Joel] You know,
they have a few more issues

1683
01:21:07,763 --> 01:21:09,732
to work through than just these, I think.

1684
01:21:09,798 --> 01:21:10,800
Yep.

1685
01:21:13,970 --> 01:21:17,607
[Tom] Well, this is the only spooky place
on the marsh. Must be it.

1686
01:21:17,673 --> 01:21:18,908
You station yourself here.

1687
01:21:18,975 --> 01:21:20,210
You men surround the house.

1688
01:21:20,277 --> 01:21:21,878
All two of you.

1689
01:21:21,945 --> 01:21:23,713
-You come with me.
-All right, Captain.

1690
01:21:25,515 --> 01:21:26,683
-Ready?
-Right.

1691
01:21:26,750 --> 01:21:27,750
[Tom] One, two, three.

1692
01:21:27,817 --> 01:21:30,487
[Joel] Check this out here.
See, it's a different door on the inside.

1693
01:21:30,553 --> 01:21:31,955
[Tom] What do you know?

1694
01:21:33,957 --> 01:21:36,893
[Tom] Eh, get these stairs.
Where the heck do they go? Nowhere.

1695
01:21:36,960 --> 01:21:38,928
I wish my bird, Polly, was here.

1696
01:21:44,033 --> 01:21:46,568
[Joel] Oh, wow!
It's a whole other room.

1697
01:21:48,838 --> 01:21:51,273
[Tom] Whoa, guess he shouldn't have
had that Mexican for lunch, eh?

1698
01:21:51,340 --> 01:21:53,042
[Joel] Oh, yeah. He's paying for it.

1699
01:21:55,912 --> 01:21:59,282
Oh, no. Now he's got
the strength of 20 heroin addicts.

1700
01:22:01,852 --> 01:22:03,687
[Joel] Honey, wake up.
You've got to see this.

1701
01:22:06,657 --> 01:22:08,925
[Tom] Payback time. Bleh.

1702
01:22:12,162 --> 01:22:14,530
Everyone keeps finding the same clues.

1703
01:22:17,200 --> 01:22:19,468
[Joel] Hey, he's got platform shoes.

1704
01:22:19,535 --> 01:22:20,870
Sly Stone style.

1705
01:22:20,937 --> 01:22:22,105
Get down.

1706
01:22:22,172 --> 01:22:24,873
You know, this is Bela's best scene,
and he's not even in it.

1707
01:22:27,177 --> 01:22:29,578
[Tom] Come here, come here.
This is what I'd do if Bela was here.

1708
01:22:39,622 --> 01:22:41,057
Oh! Lobo got back.

1709
01:22:41,658 --> 01:22:44,627
[Joel] Oh, his head looks like
a piece of ugly fruit.

1710
01:22:44,693 --> 01:22:46,997
Yuck. And so does his body.

1711
01:22:47,063 --> 01:22:49,765
[Tom] OK. OK, pal.
OK, I made some mistakes.

1712
01:22:49,832 --> 01:22:53,135
You've made some mistakes. We said
some things we shouldn't have, I know.

1713
01:22:58,040 --> 01:23:00,377
Here, hold this speaker.
[laughs]

1714
01:23:02,345 --> 01:23:04,347
[Joel] Man, that's touchy equipment.

1715
01:23:08,452 --> 01:23:09,452
[Tom] There he goes.

1716
01:23:13,657 --> 01:23:15,725
-You are my wife.
-[Tom] Goodbye, city life.

1717
01:23:19,028 --> 01:23:20,763
[Joel] Hey, yeah, thanks, honey.

1718
01:23:20,830 --> 01:23:22,565
I'll just take care of myself, here.

1719
01:23:22,665 --> 01:23:24,667
-Missed the train.
-[Tom] Ooh.

1720
01:23:27,603 --> 01:23:29,638
-Max.
-[Tom] Da-da-da-da.

1721
01:23:35,478 --> 01:23:37,313
Look, the place is on fire.

1722
01:23:37,380 --> 01:23:39,182
Let's get out of here.
We're going to get roasted.

1723
01:23:39,215 --> 01:23:41,050
[Tom] Boy, they are detectives, huh?

1724
01:23:41,683 --> 01:23:42,818
[both coughing]

1725
01:23:46,088 --> 01:23:48,123
[stammers] Captain! Captain, look.

1726
01:23:48,658 --> 01:23:50,160
[humming wedding march]

1727
01:23:50,227 --> 01:23:52,995
[Joel] That's not something
you see every day, Bela Lugosi standing,

1728
01:23:53,062 --> 01:23:54,930
carrying a lady in a wedding dress.

1729
01:23:54,997 --> 01:23:57,333
[humming wedding march]

1730
01:23:58,133 --> 01:23:59,202
What do you suppose that was?

1731
01:23:59,268 --> 01:24:01,003
Well, whatever it is, get it.

1732
01:24:01,070 --> 01:24:02,272
Oh, wait a minute, here comes Dick.

1733
01:24:02,338 --> 01:24:04,240
I'm a Grimault Warrior!

1734
01:24:04,307 --> 01:24:07,177
[humming "Wedding March"]

1735
01:24:07,243 --> 01:24:09,512
-He's got Jan!
-Get him.

1736
01:24:09,912 --> 01:24:12,882
No, don't shoot.
Might hit Jan. Let's go.

1737
01:24:15,452 --> 01:24:17,253
[Tom] Can I be in the film, too?
I'm coming.

1738
01:24:17,320 --> 01:24:19,922
[Crow humming "Wedding March"]

1739
01:24:21,590 --> 01:24:22,625
-Marty.
-Yeah.

1740
01:24:22,692 --> 01:24:25,428
-Give me your gun.
-[Joel] Just take the shirt off, Dick.

1741
01:24:25,495 --> 01:24:26,662
Gawl.

1742
01:24:27,530 --> 01:24:29,365
[Crow humming "Wedding March"]

1743
01:24:29,432 --> 01:24:30,767
Well, this is your day, dear.

1744
01:24:31,367 --> 01:24:32,902
[Tom] Ah, just set her down anywhere.

1745
01:24:33,268 --> 01:24:34,470
She got smaller.

1746
01:24:36,405 --> 01:24:39,908
[Joel] Ooh, I must have left the iron on.
Blee.

1747
01:24:39,975 --> 01:24:41,610
[laughs]

1748
01:24:46,182 --> 01:24:49,385
[Joel] I'm going to go make Plan 9,
I'll be right back.

1749
01:24:50,553 --> 01:24:51,553
[Tom] Oh, Blanche Dubois.

1750
01:24:51,620 --> 01:24:54,357
I've always depended
on the kindness of cross-dressers.

1751
01:24:54,857 --> 01:24:57,127
No! Now, get on up over that ledge.

1752
01:24:57,193 --> 01:24:59,528
-Get up over on top of him.
-That's what I was going to suggest.

1753
01:24:59,595 --> 01:25:00,997
Well, stop the chattering.
Get in action.

1754
01:25:01,063 --> 01:25:02,098
[Tom] Find my finger.

1755
01:25:05,268 --> 01:25:08,203
[Joel] Oxy-10 hates zits.

1756
01:25:08,303 --> 01:25:10,273
Now he's clear. Give it to him.

1757
01:25:13,677 --> 01:25:15,077
[Tom] You're shooting me, you dope.

1758
01:25:17,613 --> 01:25:18,715
Now I'm pissed.

1759
01:25:23,318 --> 01:25:25,288
[Joel] Oh, great, he's getting nuder.

1760
01:25:28,123 --> 01:25:31,427
[Tom] I will not be mocked. Bleh.

1761
01:25:35,298 --> 01:25:37,567
Ah, don't worry, honey,
the shots don't even match.

1762
01:25:37,633 --> 01:25:38,635
[Tom] Nice tattoo.

1763
01:25:39,835 --> 01:25:41,537
[Joel] Oh, I bet that boulder's
going to roll.

1764
01:25:41,603 --> 01:25:43,640
-[Crow] Yeah?
- [Joel] Check it out.

1765
01:25:48,645 --> 01:25:50,012
[Tom] Who is that guy?

1766
01:25:50,247 --> 01:25:52,082
Uh... Uh.

1767
01:25:53,550 --> 01:25:56,318
-Hmm? Wha--?
-[all laugh]

1768
01:25:56,385 --> 01:25:58,922
[Joel] Oh, it's Kooky,
the Crazy Clown Cop!

1769
01:25:58,988 --> 01:26:00,022
[Tom] Kooky.

1770
01:26:03,358 --> 01:26:05,995
No, keep the ripped shirt on!
You're ruining it for me!

1771
01:26:08,532 --> 01:26:09,598
[Tom] Aw, geez.

1772
01:26:10,767 --> 01:26:12,902
Come up lame.
Now I'll have to be destroyed.

1773
01:26:14,270 --> 01:26:15,572
[all] Ahh!

1774
01:26:18,575 --> 01:26:19,942
Boing, boing.

1775
01:26:20,008 --> 01:26:22,078
-Bing-boing-boing.
- [Joel] Oh, good, Dick's here.

1776
01:26:25,915 --> 01:26:27,483
Arr-rah-rah-rah-rah!

1777
01:26:33,923 --> 01:26:36,092
[all stammer in confusion]

1778
01:26:36,425 --> 01:26:38,895
[Tom] Oh, well of course, this makes sense
because it's part of the--

1779
01:26:39,395 --> 01:26:41,563
[Joel] What? No.

1780
01:26:41,630 --> 01:26:43,032
[Tom] And then there's-- Wha...?

1781
01:26:43,098 --> 01:26:45,033
[all stammer in confusion]

1782
01:26:45,100 --> 01:26:47,070
[Joel] Then, what--? Oh.

1783
01:26:47,670 --> 01:26:49,672
Oh. Uh, wha--?

1784
01:26:52,242 --> 01:26:54,443
[Joel] Well, it got him,
but what about Bela?

1785
01:26:54,510 --> 01:26:55,678
Yeah.

1786
01:26:58,347 --> 01:27:00,015
Trevor, the whisper-thin cop.

1787
01:27:04,320 --> 01:27:05,822
[screaming]

1788
01:27:05,888 --> 01:27:07,290
[Tom] Kinda grows on you, huh?

1789
01:27:13,763 --> 01:27:16,665
- [Joel] Well, calamari, anybody?
-[Tom] Ooh.

1790
01:27:19,768 --> 01:27:22,038
That was one unstable octopus.

1791
01:27:22,105 --> 01:27:26,408
[Joel] Uh, I've had enough of this, Bob.
I have a bird to get home to, excuse me.

1792
01:27:28,110 --> 01:27:29,378
[Tom] Oh, boy, how did he do that?

1793
01:27:37,320 --> 01:27:39,188
He tampered in God's domain.

1794
01:27:39,255 --> 01:27:41,690
Oh, great.
The nutty bird man in apartment 4B

1795
01:27:41,757 --> 01:27:43,660
is going to give us a religious insight.

1796
01:27:46,295 --> 01:27:47,330
-[Tom] Loser.
- [Joel] Mm-hmm.

1797
01:27:47,397 --> 01:27:48,965
[Tom] Loser. Loser.

1798
01:27:49,032 --> 01:27:50,600
Harvey B Loser.

1799
01:27:50,667 --> 01:27:52,702
George Beck Loser.

1800
01:27:52,768 --> 01:27:54,870
Paul Loser Marco.

1801
01:27:54,937 --> 01:27:57,107
Don "The Big Loser" Nagel.

1802
01:27:57,340 --> 01:28:00,008
Bud "Big Ol' Loser" Osborne.

1803
01:28:00,075 --> 01:28:02,578
John "Wanky Loser" Warren.

1804
01:28:02,645 --> 01:28:05,882
[Tom] And-- Uh,
for crying out loud-- Bah!

1805
01:28:06,182 --> 01:28:07,817
But the drunk was good. Ben Frommer.

1806
01:28:07,883 --> 01:28:09,785
-[Tom] Drunk was real good.
-[Crow] Oh, yeah.

1807
01:28:19,895 --> 01:28:21,830
Are you ready, Cambot?
OK, let's ro--

1808
01:28:21,897 --> 01:28:24,900
Oh, you know what, we should explain
what we're doing to the people, here.

1809
01:28:24,967 --> 01:28:26,768
Yeah, yeah--
What are we doing?

1810
01:28:26,835 --> 01:28:30,272
Well, we're-- Cambot believes
that he could re-edit the film

1811
01:28:30,340 --> 01:28:32,642
better than Ed Wood, Jr.
And we're going to help him.

1812
01:28:32,708 --> 01:28:34,977
Uh, b-- bu-- but, Joel,
that's not what Cambot thinks.

1813
01:28:35,043 --> 01:28:37,513
Cambot thinks that
that candy-butt, Ed Wood

1814
01:28:37,580 --> 01:28:39,682
edits like he's got his head crammed--

1815
01:28:39,782 --> 01:28:42,385
Uh, it really doesn't matter
what Cambot thinks.

1816
01:28:42,452 --> 01:28:44,587
What matters is that
we're going to be involved

1817
01:28:44,653 --> 01:28:47,223
in the re-editing of the final scene
of the film.

1818
01:28:47,290 --> 01:28:50,258
We're going to help out by playing
the role of Bela Lugosi.

1819
01:28:50,325 --> 01:28:52,962
OK, so please, I abhor you.
Places everyone.

1820
01:28:53,028 --> 01:28:55,063
Rolling, and action!

1821
01:28:55,130 --> 01:28:57,100
Now he's clear. Give it to him.

1822
01:28:58,533 --> 01:28:59,602
[gunshots]

1823
01:29:04,173 --> 01:29:05,642
[suspenseful music playing]

1824
01:29:16,452 --> 01:29:18,353
[all screaming]

1825
01:29:25,060 --> 01:29:26,895
[all] Whoa!

1826
01:29:26,962 --> 01:29:29,065
Wow, we really tampered in God's domain.

1827
01:29:29,132 --> 01:29:30,667
Yep. Live and learn, huh?

1828
01:29:30,733 --> 01:29:31,733
Well, letter?

1829
01:29:31,800 --> 01:29:35,303
Ha! Good idea. Sha-la-la.
Let's live for today.

1830
01:29:35,370 --> 01:29:41,043
This is from A1C Bill Connor
from Keflavik, Iceland.

1831
01:29:41,110 --> 01:29:43,980
Uh... quick, Crow.
What's the capital of Iceland?

1832
01:29:44,047 --> 01:29:45,415
Uh, Ice City.

1833
01:29:45,815 --> 01:29:47,883
-No, I'm sorry. That's Reykjavik.
-Oh!

1834
01:29:47,950 --> 01:29:50,553
-Reykjavik. Reykjavik.
-Reykjavik.

1835
01:29:50,620 --> 01:29:53,088
And uh, let's put that up
on still store, Cambot.

1836
01:29:53,155 --> 01:29:56,892
And we've also got two pictures here, too.

1837
01:29:56,958 --> 01:29:59,862
And let's see what Bill Connor says.

1838
01:29:59,928 --> 01:30:02,732
"Dear Joel, Tom, Crow, Gypsy,
and everyone else at MST.

1839
01:30:02,798 --> 01:30:04,867
Greetings from the land of fire and ice.

1840
01:30:04,933 --> 01:30:07,035
I'm a radio and television broadcaster

1841
01:30:07,103 --> 01:30:10,507
in the Air Force,
stationed in Keflavik, Iceland.

1842
01:30:10,573 --> 01:30:14,143
Every Thursday night is MST night
for the folks at the TV station,

1843
01:30:14,210 --> 01:30:16,012
and it's a big morale boost--"

1844
01:30:16,078 --> 01:30:18,882
They're playing our tapes on a TV s--
For us.

1845
01:30:18,982 --> 01:30:20,382
"Well, anyway, we're a little behind.

1846
01:30:20,450 --> 01:30:23,085
We're still watching last year's
Turkey Day show,

1847
01:30:23,152 --> 01:30:24,953
-but we're trying hard to catch up."
-"Catch up."

1848
01:30:25,020 --> 01:30:28,323
"I've included some pictures of Iceland.
One of golf's--" Show that on there.

1849
01:30:28,390 --> 01:30:29,525
-[Crow] Pretty.
-[Joel] Yeah.

1850
01:30:29,592 --> 01:30:35,130
"And one of Althing, the sight of
the oldest democracy in the world."

1851
01:30:35,197 --> 01:30:36,498
-[all] Hmm.
-Well, what do you know?

1852
01:30:36,565 --> 01:30:39,302
What do you know?
Banachek is the capital of Iceland.

1853
01:30:39,368 --> 01:30:41,970
-No, it's Reykjavik.
-Reklavik.

1854
01:30:42,037 --> 01:30:44,173
-Reyk-- Ja--
-Reyk-- Ja--

1855
01:30:44,240 --> 01:30:45,942
-Vik. Reykjavik.
-Vik. Reyklavik.

1856
01:30:46,008 --> 01:30:47,810
-Reyk--
-Rikkles. Reklek.

1857
01:30:48,310 --> 01:30:50,278
Lobo.

1858
01:30:50,345 --> 01:30:51,747
Lobo!

1859
01:30:53,648 --> 01:30:54,783
[panting]

1860
01:30:59,022 --> 01:31:00,823
[whispering] I'm a dead man.

1861
01:31:02,592 --> 01:31:05,562
[theme music playing]

1862
01:32:05,487 --> 01:32:07,323
[suspenseful music playing]

